window.POP_REGISTER_CONTENT("colossians", {"intro":{"type":"masterIntroduction","book":"Colossians","chapters":4,"bigPicture":{"subtitle":"The Christ Who Needs Nothing Added","paragraphs":["Colossians is a letter to a small church in a fading town that its author had never visited. Colossae sat in the Lycus valley in Phrygia, in what is now western Turkey. Centuries earlier it had been a significant city on the trade road from the coast to the east, known for a dark red wool that took its name from the town. By Paul's day it had been overshadowed by its two neighbours, wealthy Laodicea about ten miles away and the spa city of Hierapolis a little further off. Its site has never been fully excavated; much of it is still an unexcavated mound. If any church in the New Testament looked unimportant, it was this one. And Paul wrote to it one of the most exalted descriptions of Jesus Christ ever put into words.","Paul had not founded this church himself. During his two years of teaching in Ephesus, “all those who lived in Asia heard the word of the Lord Jesus” (Acts 19:10), and one of the people who carried the gospel inland was a Colossian named Epaphras, “our beloved fellow servant” (1:7). He planted the church in his home town and probably those in Laodicea and Hierapolis too (4:13). Now Epaphras had travelled to Paul, who was in prison, with news: the church was growing in faith and love — but it was being drawn toward a teaching that threatened to pull it away from Christ.","That teaching is never described in full, and scholars still debate its exact shape. But Paul's answers let us sketch it. It called itself “philosophy” (2:8). It involved food rules, festivals, new moons and Sabbaths (2:16). It encouraged the “worshiping of the angels,” claimed visions, and practised a show of humility and “severity to the body” (2:18, 23). It may have combined Jewish observances with local religious ideas about spiritual powers. Its message, in effect, was: Christ is a good beginning, but to reach real spiritual fullness you need more — more rules, more visions, more mediators, more discipline. Paul's answer is the whole letter in one sentence: “For in him all the fullness of the Deity dwells bodily, and in him you are made full” (2:9–10).","So Colossians is a letter about the supremacy and sufficiency of Christ. Chapter 1 contains a hymn as soaring as the one in Philippians 2: Christ is “the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of all creation. For by him all things were created… He is before all things, and in him all things are held together” (1:15–17). Chapter 2 dismantles every attempt to add to Him. Chapter 3 turns from doctrine to life: “If then you were raised together with Christ, seek the things that are above” (3:1), and shows what that looks like in speech, in forgiveness, in worship, in marriage, in work. Chapter 4 ends with prayer, wise conversation with outsiders, and a list of names — including Onesimus, a runaway slave now called “the faithful and beloved brother” (4:9), whose story is told in the letter to Philemon that travelled with this one.","You will notice that Colossians often sounds like Ephesians. They were written from the same imprisonment, probably carried by the same messenger, Tychicus (4:7; Ephesians 6:21), and share much of their language. (Some scholars question whether Paul himself wrote Colossians; the letter claims his name, carries his signature in 4:18, and is woven into his life through the same people named in Philemon, and the strongest reading is that it is his.) But where Ephesians lifts your eyes to the church as Christ's body, Colossians lifts your eyes to the Head. If you have ever felt that your faith needed something more — a deeper experience, a stricter discipline, a secret insight, a spiritual technique — Colossians was written for you. Its answer is not more of something else. It is more of Christ, because in Him you already have everything."]},"bridge":{"fromBook":"Philippians","intro":"The Holy Spirit placed Colossians directly after Philippians. Both were written by Paul in chains, very probably in the same imprisonment, and both centre on a great hymn to Christ. But they look at Him from opposite directions:","points":[{"title":"From a Church He Loved to a Church He Had Never Seen","body":"Philippians was written to Paul's oldest friends in Europe — the church of Lydia and the jailer, who had supported him from the first day. Colossians is written to people who had never seen his face (2:1). He knows them only through Epaphras, who brought him their news. And yet he writes, “For I desire to have you know how greatly I struggle for you, and for those at Laodicea, and for as many as have not seen my face in the flesh” (2:1). Philippians shows the love of an apostle for the church he planted. Colossians shows the love of an apostle for a church he had only prayed for — proof that the body of Christ is bound together not by acquaintance but by the Head."},{"title":"From the Descent to the Supremacy","body":"Philippians 2 showed you Christ going down: He “existing in the form of God, didn’t consider equality with God a thing to be grasped, but emptied himself, taking the form of a servant” (Philippians 2:6–7). Colossians 1 shows you how high He was before He came down, and how high He is now: “who is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of all creation. For by him all things were created, in the heavens and on the earth, things visible and things invisible” (1:15–16). Philippians gave you the humility of the Son. Colossians gives you the majesty of the Son. You need both, because the One who washed feet is the One who holds the universe together."},{"title":"From “No Confidence in the Flesh” to “Made Full in Him”","body":"In Philippians 3 Paul warned against teachers who added circumcision to Christ, and declared, “we are the circumcision, who worship God in the Spirit, and rejoice in Christ Jesus, and have no confidence in the flesh” (Philippians 3:3). In Colossae the additions were different — philosophy, angel worship, visions, food laws and harsh treatment of the body — but the error had the same root: Christ is not enough. Paul's answer in Colossians takes the next step. Not only should you put no confidence in the flesh; you have no need of anything beyond Christ, because “in him all the fullness of the Deity dwells bodily, and in him you are made full” (2:9–10). Philippians told you what to throw away. Colossians tells you why you will never miss it."},{"title":"From Citizenship to Mindset","body":"Philippians warned against those “who think about earthly things” and declared, “For our citizenship is in heaven” (Philippians 3:19–20). Colossians turns that citizenship into a daily command, using the very same verb: “Set your mind on the things that are above, not on the things that are on the earth” (3:2). The word is φρονέω (phronéō, G5426), the key verb of Philippians — the “mind of Christ.” And Colossians tells you why you can: “For you died, and your life is hidden with Christ in God” (3:3). Philippians told you where your passport is issued. Colossians teaches you to think like a citizen of that city every day — in your speech, your relationships, your work and your worship."}]},"coreTheme":{"title":"In Him All the Fullness Dwells — and in Him You Are Made Full","anchorRef":"Colossians 2:9–10","paragraphs":["“For in him all the fullness of the Deity dwells bodily, and in him you are made full, who is the head of all principality and power” (2:9–10). This is the sentence the whole letter turns on. Notice the two halves. First, who Christ is: in Him dwells all the πλήρωμα (plḗrōma, G4138), the fullness, of θεότης (theótēs, G2320), deity itself — not a share of divine power, not a high rank among spiritual beings, but the whole being of God — and it dwells σωματικῶς (sōmatikōs, G4985), bodily, in a human body. Second, who you are in Him: πεπληρωμένοι — you have been filled, made full, brought to completeness. The same root, πλήρης. All the fullness is in Him, and you are in Him, so you are full.","That truth demolishes every teaching that says Christ is the beginning but not the end. The Colossian teachers offered fullness through additions: visions, angelic mediators, special days, strict rules about food and the body. Paul answers that every one of those things is “a shadow of the things to come; but the body is Christ’s” (2:17). Why would you go back to the shadow when you have the substance? Why would you seek mediators when you have the Head of every power (2:10)? Why would you look for hidden wisdom when in Christ “are all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge hidden” (2:3)?","The same truth reshapes daily life. Because you are complete in Christ, you do not need to earn spiritual status by rules — “Don’t handle, nor taste, nor touch” (2:21) — which “aren’t of any value against the indulgence of the flesh” (2:23). Instead, you set your mind on Christ above, put to death what belongs to the earthly life, and put on the new self: compassion, kindness, humility, forgiveness, and above all love (3:5–14). Transformation does not come from adding regulations to Christ. It comes from living out what you already have in Him.","This theme echoes across the New Testament. John wrote that the Word who “was God” became flesh, and “From his fullness we all received grace upon grace” (John 1:16). Hebrews calls the Son “the radiance of his glory, the very image of his substance, and upholding all things by the word of his power” (Hebrews 1:3). And Paul's own words to the Ephesians, written in the same season, speak of “the fullness of him who fills all in all” (Ephesians 1:23). As you read Colossians, keep asking one question: What am I looking for outside of Christ that I already have in Him?"]},"truths":{"intro":"As we journey through these 4 chapters, the Holy Spirit wants to anchor these truths in your spirit:","items":[{"title":"Christ Is Supreme Over Everything","body":"Colossians 1:15–20 is one of the highest statements of who Jesus is in all of Scripture. He is “the image of the invisible God” — the visible face of the God no one has seen. He is the Creator of everything, including every spiritual power: “all things have been created through him, and for him” (1:16). He holds the universe together (1:17). He is the head of the church and the first to rise from the dead, “that in all things he might have the preeminence” (1:18). And through His blood shed on the cross He is reconciling all things to God (1:20). There is no power, no person, no circumstance in your life that is not under Him. If Christ is preeminent in all things, the question is whether He is preeminent in yours."},{"title":"You Are Complete in Him — Stop Looking for More","body":"“And in him you are made full” (2:10). The Colossians were being told they needed something extra to reach true spirituality. Every generation has its version: a second experience, a special technique, a set of rules, a hidden insight, a spiritual elite. Paul's answer is that all of these are shadows, and Christ is the substance (2:17). When you received Christ, you received everything. You were buried with Him, raised with Him, forgiven, and set free from every hostile power, because He “made a show of them openly, triumphing over them” at the cross (2:15). You do not need to add to Christ. You need to grow deeper into Him: “As therefore you received Christ Jesus, the Lord, walk in him” (2:6)."},{"title":"Your Life Is Hidden With Christ — So Live It Out Everywhere","body":"“For you died, and your life is hidden with Christ in God” (3:3). Because your real life is with Christ above, Paul calls you to put off the old self — sexual immorality, greed, anger, slander, lying — and put on the new: “a heart of compassion, kindness, lowliness, humility, and perseverance; bearing with one another, and forgiving each other… even as Christ forgave you, so you also do” (3:12–13). And he takes that new life into every room: worship, marriage, parenting, work, prayer and conversation with outsiders. “Whatever you do, in word or in deed, do all in the name of the Lord Jesus” (3:17). Colossians will not let you keep Christ in a compartment. If He holds all things together, He must hold your whole life together too."}]},"preparation":{"intro":"Before we step into Chapter 1, prepare your spirit with these reflections:","items":[{"title":"What Are You Adding?","body":"Be honest: is there something you have quietly started to believe you need in addition to Christ to be a “real” or mature believer? A particular experience, a set of rules, a spiritual technique, a teacher, a diet, a form of discipline, a special insight? None of these may be wrong in themselves. But if you are relying on any of them for fullness, Colossians will gently and firmly take it out of your hands. Write it down before you begin, and ask the Spirit to show you, as you read, that Christ is enough."},{"title":"How Big Is Your Christ?","body":"Many believers know Jesus as Saviour and friend, but seldom think of Him as the One through whom and for whom the whole universe was made, and in whom it holds together. Before you read chapter 1, sit for a few minutes and think about the largest thing you know — the night sky, the ocean, the vastness of space — and then remember that Colossians says “all things have been created through him, and for him. He is before all things, and in him all things are held together.” Ask God to enlarge your vision of Christ, because the size of your Christ will determine the size of your faith, your worship and your peace."}]},"prayerOfActivation":"“Father, I come to this letter asking You to enlarge my vision of Your Son. Show me Jesus as Colossians shows Him: the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of all creation, the One through whom and for whom all things were made, the One who holds all things together, the head of the church, the firstborn from the dead, preeminent in everything. I confess that my Christ has often been too small, and that I have looked for fullness in other places. Where I have tried to add to Him — with rules, experiences, techniques, or my own discipline — show me that these are shadows, and that the substance is Christ. Teach me that in Him all the fullness of God dwells bodily, and that in Him I have been made full. As I received Christ Jesus the Lord, let me walk in Him, rooted and built up in Him and established in the faith, overflowing with thanksgiving. Lift my mind to the things above, where Christ is seated. Hide my life with Him in You. And then let that hidden life come out into every part of my days — my words, my relationships, my work, my prayers — so that whatever I do, in word or deed, I do it all in the name of the Lord Jesus. In His name, Amen.”"},"chapters":[{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Colossians","chapter":1,"chapterTitle":"The Image of the Invisible God — Supreme in Creation, Supreme in Redemption","themeTitle":"“That in All Things He Might Have the Preeminence”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Paul, an apostle of Christ Jesus through the will of God, and Timothy our brother,","understanding":"Paul writes as “an apostle of Christ Jesus through the will of God” — the authority he needs with a church that had never met him and was being pulled toward other teachers. Timothy, “our brother,” stands beside him, as in Philippians.","words":[{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"apostle"},{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"}]},{"v":2,"text":"to the saints and faithful brothers in Christ at Colossae: Grace to you and peace from God our Father, and the Lord Jesus Christ.","understanding":"To the saints and πιστοί ἀδελφοί — faithful brothers — in Christ at Κολοσσαί. They are “in Christ” and they are “at Colossae”: two addresses at once. Every believer lives in a town on earth and in Christ in heaven (3:3).","words":[{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G2857","hebrew":"Κολοσσαί","translit":"Kolossaí","english":"Colossae"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"}]},{"v":3,"text":"We give thanks to God the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, praying always for you,","understanding":"Paul gives thanks to God, “the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ,” whenever he prays for them. He had never seen their faces, but he had prayed for them by name.","words":[{"strongs":"G2168","hebrew":"εὐχαριστέω","translit":"eucharistéō","english":"give thanks"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"},{"strongs":"G4336","hebrew":"προσεύχομαι","translit":"proseúchomai","english":"praying"}]},{"v":4,"text":"having heard of your faith in Christ Jesus, and of the love which you have toward all the saints,","understanding":"Two things Paul heard about them: their πίστις in Christ Jesus, and their ἀγάπη toward all the saints. Faith upward, love outward. They are the signs of a real church.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"}]},{"v":5,"text":"because of the hope which is laid up for you in the heavens, of which you heard before in the word of the truth of the Good News,","understanding":"And the source of both: the ἐλπίς — hope — ἀποκειμένην, laid up, stored, reserved, for them in the heavens. Faith and love grow out of a secure hope. Because their future was kept safe in heaven, they could trust God and love people now. They heard of it ἐν τῷ λόγῳ τῆς ἀληθείας — in the word of the truth, the gospel.","words":[{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"},{"strongs":"G606","hebrew":"ἀπόκειμαι","translit":"apókeimai","english":"laid up"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heavens"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"},{"strongs":"G2098","hebrew":"εὐαγγέλιον","translit":"euangélion","english":"Good News"}]},{"v":6,"text":"which has come to you; even as it is in all the world and is bearing fruit and growing, as it does in you also, since the day you heard and knew the grace of God in truth;","understanding":"The gospel has come to them, “even as it is in all the world,” καρποφορούμενον καὶ αὐξανόμενον — bearing fruit and growing. Like a living plant, the gospel both produces fruit and spreads. The Colossian teachers offered something exclusive; Paul points to a gospel that is the same everywhere and is growing everywhere.","words":[{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G2592","hebrew":"καρποφορέω","translit":"karpophoréō","english":"bearing fruit"},{"strongs":"G837","hebrew":"αὐξάνω","translit":"auxánō","english":"growing"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"knew"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"}]},{"v":7,"text":"even as you learned of Epaphras our beloved fellow servant, who is a faithful servant of Christ on our behalf,","understanding":"They learned it from Ἐπαφρᾶς — Epaphras, a Colossian (4:12), Paul's ἀγαπητὸς σύνδουλος, beloved fellow slave, and a faithful διάκονος of Christ. Paul affirms that what Epaphras taught them was the true gospel. They do not need a different one.","words":[{"strongs":"G3129","hebrew":"μανθάνω","translit":"manthánō","english":"learned"},{"strongs":"G1889","hebrew":"Ἐπαφρᾶς","translit":"Epaphrâs","english":"Epaphras"},{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G4889","hebrew":"σύνδουλος","translit":"sýndoulos","english":"fellow servant"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G1249","hebrew":"διάκονος","translit":"diákonos","english":"servant"}]},{"v":8,"text":"who also declared to us your love in the Spirit.","understanding":"Epaphras brought Paul news of their ἀγάπη ἐν πνεύματι — love in the Spirit. This is the only clear mention of the Holy Spirit in Colossians, and it is attached to love. The Spirit's clearest fingerprint is not a vision; it is love.","words":[{"strongs":"G1213","hebrew":"δηλόω","translit":"dēlóō","english":"declared"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"}]},{"v":9,"text":"For this cause, we also, since the day we heard this, don’t cease praying and making requests for you, that you may be filled with the knowledge of his will in all spiritual wisdom and understanding,","understanding":"Paul has not stopped praying since the day he heard. His request: that they be πληρόω — filled — with the ἐπίγνωσις, the full and accurate knowledge, of God's will, in all σοφία (wisdom) and σύνεσις (understanding, putting things together) πνευματική. The false teachers promised deeper knowledge and fullness. Paul prays for true fullness — and it comes from God, not from secret teachings.","words":[{"strongs":"G4336","hebrew":"προσεύχομαι","translit":"proseúchomai","english":"praying"},{"strongs":"G4137","hebrew":"πληρόω","translit":"plēróō","english":"filled"},{"strongs":"G1922","hebrew":"ἐπίγνωσις","translit":"epígnōsis","english":"knowledge"},{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"},{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G4907","hebrew":"σύνεσις","translit":"sýnesis","english":"understanding"},{"strongs":"G4152","hebrew":"πνευματικός","translit":"pneumatikós","english":"spiritual"}]},{"v":10,"text":"that you may walk worthily of the Lord, to please him in all respects, bearing fruit in every good work, and increasing in the knowledge of God;","understanding":"The purpose of knowledge is a life. Περιπατῆσαι ἀξίως τοῦ κυρίου — walk worthily of the Lord — εἰς πᾶσαν ἀρεσκείαν, to please Him in every way. Bearing fruit in every good work, and αὐξάνω — growing — in the knowledge of God. Knowledge leads to obedience, and obedience leads to deeper knowledge. They grow in a cycle.","words":[{"strongs":"G4043","hebrew":"περιπατέω","translit":"peripatéō","english":"walk"},{"strongs":"G516","hebrew":"ἀξίως","translit":"axíōs","english":"worthily"},{"strongs":"G699","hebrew":"ἀρεσκεία","translit":"areskeía","english":"please"},{"strongs":"G2592","hebrew":"καρποφορέω","translit":"karpophoréō","english":"bearing fruit"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"work"},{"strongs":"G837","hebrew":"αὐξάνω","translit":"auxánō","english":"increasing"},{"strongs":"G1922","hebrew":"ἐπίγνωσις","translit":"epígnōsis","english":"knowledge"}]},{"v":11,"text":"strengthened with all power, according to the might of his glory, for all endurance and perseverance with joy;","understanding":"Power words stack up: ἐν πάσῃ δυνάμει δυναμούμενοι — empowered with all power — κατὰ τὸ κράτος τῆς δόξης αὐτοῦ, according to the might of His glory. And what is all that power for? Not spectacular feats, but εἰς πᾶσαν ὑπομονὴν καὶ μακροθυμίαν — endurance (holding up under circumstances) and patience (holding up under people) — μετὰ χαρᾶς, with joy. God's mightiest power is often seen in a believer who keeps going, gladly.","words":[{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G1412","hebrew":"δυναμόω","translit":"dynamóō","english":"strengthened"},{"strongs":"G2904","hebrew":"κράτος","translit":"krátos","english":"might"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G5281","hebrew":"ὑπομονή","translit":"hypomonḗ","english":"endurance"},{"strongs":"G3115","hebrew":"μακροθυμία","translit":"makrothymía","english":"perseverance"},{"strongs":"G5479","hebrew":"χαρά","translit":"chará","english":"joy"}]},{"v":12,"text":"giving thanks to the Father, who made us fit to be partakers of the inheritance of the saints in light;","understanding":"Εὐχαριστοῦντες — giving thanks — to the Father, who ἱκανόω — made sufficient, qualified — us for τὴν μερίδα τοῦ κλήρου, the share of the inheritance, of the saints in the light. The image is Israel dividing the promised land by lot. You did not qualify yourself; the Father qualified you. You need no further qualification from any teacher.","words":[{"strongs":"G2168","hebrew":"εὐχαριστέω","translit":"eucharistéō","english":"giving thanks"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"},{"strongs":"G2427","hebrew":"ἱκανόω","translit":"hikanóō","english":"made fit"},{"strongs":"G3310","hebrew":"μερίς","translit":"merís","english":"partakers"},{"strongs":"G2819","hebrew":"κλῆρος","translit":"klēros","english":"inheritance"},{"strongs":"G5457","hebrew":"φῶς","translit":"phōs","english":"light"}]},{"v":13,"text":"who delivered us out of the power of darkness, and translated us into the Kingdom of the Son of his love;","understanding":"Two decisive verbs. He ῥύομαι — rescued, dragged out — us from the ἐξουσία τοῦ σκότους, the authority of darkness. And He μεθίστημι — transferred, relocated — us into the kingdom of the Son of His love. The word was used of moving an entire population from one country to another. You have been moved, permanently, from one kingdom into another.","words":[{"strongs":"G4506","hebrew":"ῥύομαι","translit":"rhýomai","english":"delivered"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G4655","hebrew":"σκότος","translit":"skótos","english":"darkness"},{"strongs":"G3179","hebrew":"μεθίστημι","translit":"methístēmi","english":"translated"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"Kingdom"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"}]},{"v":14,"text":"in whom we have our redemption, the forgiveness of our sins;","understanding":"In Him we have — present tense, possessed now — τὴν ἀπολύτρωσιν, the redemption, release by payment of a ransom, τὴν ἄφεσιν τῶν ἁμαρτιῶν, the forgiveness, the sending away, of sins. The Colossians did not need further rituals to deal with sin. It had already been dealt with. (The phrase “through his blood,” in some later manuscripts here, is in Ephesians 1:7.)","words":[{"strongs":"G629","hebrew":"ἀπολύτρωσις","translit":"apolýtrōsis","english":"redemption"},{"strongs":"G859","hebrew":"ἄφεσις","translit":"áphesis","english":"forgiveness"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"}]},{"v":15,"text":"who is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of all creation.","understanding":"Now the great hymn begins. Christ is the εἰκὼν τοῦ θεοῦ τοῦ ἀοράτου — the image of the invisible God. No one has ever seen God; in Christ, the invisible God is made visible. And He is πρωτότοκος πάσης κτίσεως — firstborn over all creation. Firstborn here means rank, not origin: the firstborn son held the place of honour and inheritance (God calls David His firstborn in Psalm 89:27). The next verse makes this unmistakable: He is not part of creation; He is its Creator.","words":[{"strongs":"G1504","hebrew":"εἰκών","translit":"eikṓn","english":"image"},{"strongs":"G517","hebrew":"ἀόρατος","translit":"aóratos","english":"invisible"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4416","hebrew":"πρωτότοκος","translit":"prōtótokos","english":"firstborn"},{"strongs":"G2937","hebrew":"κτίσις","translit":"ktísis","english":"creation"}]},{"v":16,"text":"For by him all things were created, in the heavens and on the earth, things visible and things invisible, whether thrones or dominions or principalities or powers; all things have been created through him, and for him.","understanding":"Ὅτι ἐν αὐτῷ ἐκτίσθη τὰ πάντα — because in Him all things were created. Everything: in heaven and on earth, visible and invisible. And Paul names the very spiritual powers the false teachers revered: θρόνοι (thrones), κυριότητες (dominions), ἀρχαί (rulers), ἐξουσίαι (authorities). Christ made them all. Then the three prepositions: ἐν αὐτῷ (in Him), δι’ αὐτοῦ (through Him), εἰς αὐτόν (for Him). He is the sphere, the agent and the goal of all creation. Why would you worship angels He created?","words":[{"strongs":"G2936","hebrew":"κτίζω","translit":"ktízō","english":"created"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heavens"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G3707","hebrew":"ὁρατός","translit":"horatós","english":"visible"},{"strongs":"G517","hebrew":"ἀόρατος","translit":"aóratos","english":"invisible"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"thrones"},{"strongs":"G2963","hebrew":"κυριότης","translit":"kyriótēs","english":"dominions"},{"strongs":"G746","hebrew":"ἀρχή","translit":"archḗ","english":"principalities"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"powers"}]},{"v":17,"text":"He is before all things, and in him all things are held together.","understanding":"Αὐτός ἐστιν πρὸ πάντων — He is before all things, in time and in rank. Καὶ τὰ πάντα ἐν αὐτῷ συνέστηκεν — and in Him all things hold together, cohere, are sustained. The universe does not run on its own. Every atom holds together because the Son sustains it (Hebrews 1:3, “upholding all things by the word of his power”).","words":[{"strongs":"G4253","hebrew":"πρό","translit":"pró","english":"before"},{"strongs":"G4921","hebrew":"συνιστάω","translit":"synistáō","english":"held together"}]},{"v":18,"text":"He is the head of the body, the assembly, who is the beginning, the firstborn from the dead; that in all things he might have the preeminence.","understanding":"The hymn turns from creation to the church. He is the κεφαλή — head — of the σῶμα, the body, the church. He is the ἀρχή — the beginning, the source — and πρωτότοκος ἐκ τῶν νεκρῶν, firstborn from the dead, the first to rise to resurrection life. So that ἐν πᾶσιν αὐτὸς πρωτεύων — in everything He might be first, hold the preeminence. That word, πρωτεύω, appears only here in the New Testament.","words":[{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"head"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"},{"strongs":"G746","hebrew":"ἀρχή","translit":"archḗ","english":"beginning"},{"strongs":"G4416","hebrew":"πρωτότοκος","translit":"prōtótokos","english":"firstborn"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G4409","hebrew":"πρωτεύω","translit":"prōteúō","english":"have the preeminence"}]},{"v":19,"text":"For all the fullness was pleased to dwell in him;","understanding":"Ἐν αὐτῷ εὐδόκησεν πᾶν τὸ πλήρωμα κατοικῆσαι — in Him all the fullness was pleased to dwell. Πλήρωμα may have been a favourite term of the false teachers, describing the totality of divine powers spread among many beings. Paul says: all of it dwells in one Person. Κατοικέω means to settle down permanently, to make a home, not to visit.","words":[{"strongs":"G2106","hebrew":"εὐδοκέω","translit":"eudokéō","english":"pleased"},{"strongs":"G4138","hebrew":"πλήρωμα","translit":"plḗrōma","english":"fullness"},{"strongs":"G2730","hebrew":"κατοικέω","translit":"katoikéō","english":"dwell"}]},{"v":20,"text":"and through him to reconcile all things to himself, by him, whether things on the earth, or things in the heavens, having made peace through the blood of his cross.","understanding":"And through Him, God was pleased ἀποκαταλλάσσω — to reconcile fully, to restore to a right relationship — all things to Himself, on earth and in heaven, εἰρηνοποιήσας — having made peace — through the αἷμα τοῦ σταυροῦ, the blood of His cross. The One who created everything also restores everything, and He does it not by power alone but by blood.","words":[{"strongs":"G604","hebrew":"ἀποκαταλλάσσω","translit":"apokatallássō","english":"reconcile"},{"strongs":"G1517","hebrew":"εἰρηνοποιέω","translit":"eirēnopoiéō","english":"made peace"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G4716","hebrew":"σταυρός","translit":"staurós","english":"cross"}]},{"v":21,"text":"You, being in past times alienated and enemies in your mind in your evil deeds,","understanding":"Now Paul applies it to them. Once they were ἀπηλλοτριωμένοι — estranged, alienated — and ἐχθροί, enemies, τῇ διανοίᾳ — in their mind, their way of thinking — shown in evil deeds. The problem was not only bad behaviour but a hostile mind.","words":[{"strongs":"G526","hebrew":"ἀπαλλοτριόω","translit":"apallotrióō","english":"alienated"},{"strongs":"G2190","hebrew":"ἐχθρός","translit":"echthrós","english":"enemies"},{"strongs":"G1271","hebrew":"διάνοια","translit":"diánoia","english":"mind"},{"strongs":"G4190","hebrew":"πονηρός","translit":"ponērós","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"deeds"}]},{"v":22,"text":"yet now he has reconciled in the body of his flesh through death, to present you holy and without defect and blameless before him,","understanding":"“Yet now” — νυνὶ δέ — He has reconciled you ἐν τῷ σώματι τῆς σαρκὸς αὐτοῦ, in His physical body of flesh, through death. This phrase is a direct answer to anyone who thought salvation was purely spiritual or that the body did not matter: Christ reconciled you in a real body, by a real death. The goal: to παρίστημι — present — you ἁγίους, ἀμώμους, ἀνεγκλήτους: holy, unblemished like a perfect sacrifice, and beyond accusation, before Him.","words":[{"strongs":"G3568","hebrew":"νῦν","translit":"nŷn","english":"now"},{"strongs":"G604","hebrew":"ἀποκαταλλάσσω","translit":"apokatallássō","english":"reconciled"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G3936","hebrew":"παρίστημι","translit":"parístēmi","english":"present"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G299","hebrew":"ἄμωμος","translit":"ámōmos","english":"without defect"},{"strongs":"G410","hebrew":"ἀνέγκλητος","translit":"anénklētos","english":"blameless"}]},{"v":23,"text":"if it is so that you continue in the faith, grounded and steadfast, and not moved away from the hope of the Good News which you heard, which is being proclaimed in all creation under heaven; of which I, Paul, was made a servant.","understanding":"The condition: εἴ γε ἐπιμένετε — if indeed you continue — in the faith, τεθεμελιωμένοι (founded, built on a foundation) and ἑδραῖοι (settled, steadfast), and μὴ μετακινούμενοι, not being shifted away from the hope of the gospel. Perseverance is the evidence of genuine faith. The false teachers were trying to move them; Paul calls them to stay put on the foundation.","words":[{"strongs":"G1961","hebrew":"ἐπιμένω","translit":"epiménō","english":"continue"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G2311","hebrew":"θεμελιόω","translit":"themelióō","english":"grounded"},{"strongs":"G1476","hebrew":"ἑδραῖος","translit":"hedraîos","english":"steadfast"},{"strongs":"G3334","hebrew":"μετακινέω","translit":"metakinéō","english":"moved away"},{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"},{"strongs":"G2937","hebrew":"κτίσις","translit":"ktísis","english":"creation"},{"strongs":"G1249","hebrew":"διάκονος","translit":"diákonos","english":"servant"}]},{"v":24,"text":"Now I rejoice in my sufferings for your sake, and fill up on my part that which is lacking of the afflictions of Christ in my flesh for his body’s sake, which is the assembly;","understanding":"One of the most debated verses in Paul. He rejoices in his sufferings for them, and ἀνταναπληρῶ — fills up in turn — τὰ ὑστερήματα τῶν θλίψεων τοῦ Χριστοῦ, “what is lacking of the afflictions of Christ.” This cannot mean that Christ's atoning sufferings were insufficient; the whole letter says His work is complete (2:14). Θλῖψις is never used in the New Testament of Christ's atoning death. It refers to the afflictions that come on Christ's body, the church, as it carries the gospel into the world. Paul willingly takes his share for their sake.","words":[{"strongs":"G5463","hebrew":"χαίρω","translit":"chaírō","english":"rejoice"},{"strongs":"G3804","hebrew":"πάθημα","translit":"páthēma","english":"sufferings"},{"strongs":"G5303","hebrew":"ὑστέρημα","translit":"hystérēma","english":"lacking"},{"strongs":"G2347","hebrew":"θλῖψις","translit":"thlîpsis","english":"afflictions"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"}]},{"v":25,"text":"of which I was made a servant, according to the stewardship of God which was given me toward you, to fulfill the word of God,","understanding":"Paul became a διάκονος of the church according to the οἰκονομία — the stewardship, the household management — of God given to him, to πληρόω the word of God: to fulfil it, to bring it to its full extent, by preaching it to the Gentiles.","words":[{"strongs":"G1249","hebrew":"διάκονος","translit":"diákonos","english":"servant"},{"strongs":"G3622","hebrew":"οἰκονομία","translit":"oikonomía","english":"stewardship"},{"strongs":"G4137","hebrew":"πληρόω","translit":"plēróō","english":"fulfill"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"}]},{"v":26,"text":"the mystery which has been hidden for ages and generations. But now it has been revealed to his saints,","understanding":"That word is the μυστήριον — the mystery — ἀποκεκρυμμένον, hidden, for ages and generations, but now φανερόω — made visible, revealed — to His saints. The false teachers offered secrets to a spiritual elite. Paul says God's mystery has been revealed to all His people.","words":[{"strongs":"G3466","hebrew":"μυστήριον","translit":"mystḗrion","english":"mystery"},{"strongs":"G613","hebrew":"ἀποκρύπτω","translit":"apokrýptō","english":"hidden"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"ages"},{"strongs":"G1074","hebrew":"γενεά","translit":"geneá","english":"generations"},{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"}]},{"v":27,"text":"to whom God was pleased to make known what are the riches of the glory of this mystery among the Gentiles, which is Christ in you, the hope of glory;","understanding":"And what is the mystery? God wanted to make known the πλοῦτος, the riches, of its glory among the ἔθνη, the Gentiles: “Christ in you, the hope of glory.” The stunning secret hidden for centuries is that the Messiah of Israel would live inside Gentile believers. Not Christ plus rules, not Christ plus angels — Christ in you. And He is your ἐλπὶς τῆς δόξης, the guarantee of future glory.","words":[{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"pleased"},{"strongs":"G1107","hebrew":"γνωρίζω","translit":"gnōrízō","english":"make known"},{"strongs":"G4149","hebrew":"πλοῦτος","translit":"ploûtos","english":"riches"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G3466","hebrew":"μυστήριον","translit":"mystḗrion","english":"mystery"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"Gentiles"},{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"}]},{"v":28,"text":"whom we proclaim, admonishing every man and teaching every man in all wisdom, that we may present every man perfect in Christ Jesus;","understanding":"Him we καταγγέλλω — proclaim — νουθετοῦντες (warning, putting sense into minds) and διδάσκοντες (teaching) πάντα ἄνθρωπον — every person, three times in one verse — ἐν πάσῃ σοφίᾳ, so that we may present every person τέλειον, mature, complete, in Christ. Against an elitist teaching that offered fullness to a few, Paul insists: every person, every person, every person.","words":[{"strongs":"G2605","hebrew":"καταγγέλλω","translit":"katangéllō","english":"proclaim"},{"strongs":"G3560","hebrew":"νουθετέω","translit":"nouthetéō","english":"admonishing"},{"strongs":"G1321","hebrew":"διδάσκω","translit":"didáskō","english":"teaching"},{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G3936","hebrew":"παρίστημι","translit":"parístēmi","english":"present"},{"strongs":"G5046","hebrew":"τέλειος","translit":"téleios","english":"perfect"}]},{"v":29,"text":"for which I also labor, striving according to his working, which works in me mightily.","understanding":"For this Paul κοπιάω — labours to exhaustion — ἀγωνιζόμενος, struggling like an athlete in a contest, “according to his ἐνέργεια, his working, which ἐνεργέω — works — in me ἐν δυνάμει, with power.” Paul's effort and God's energy are not rivals. He works hard because God works powerfully in him.","words":[{"strongs":"G2872","hebrew":"κοπιάω","translit":"kopiáō","english":"labor"},{"strongs":"G75","hebrew":"ἀγωνίζομαι","translit":"agōnízomai","english":"striving"},{"strongs":"G1753","hebrew":"ἐνέργεια","translit":"enérgeia","english":"working"},{"strongs":"G1754","hebrew":"ἐνεργέω","translit":"energéō","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"mightily"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Face of the Invisible God","verseRef":"v. 15","image":"Picture someone born blind who has heard about the sun all her life. People have described it — warm, bright, golden — but she has never seen it. Now picture her eyes being opened one morning, and the first thing she sees is the sunrise flooding the sky. All the descriptions suddenly make sense, and she realises that none of them were enough. That is what Colossians 1:15 says Christ is: “the image of the invisible God.” For all of history, humanity had heard descriptions of God. In Jesus, God showed His face.","meaning":"The word εἰκών means an image, a portrait, an exact representation. Jesus said, “He who has seen me has seen the Father” (John 14:9), and John wrote, “No one has seen God at any time. The one and only Son, who is in the bosom of the Father, he has declared him” (John 1:18). If you want to know what God is like — whether He is kind or harsh, near or distant, patient or quick to condemn — you do not have to guess or rely on visions and intermediaries, as the Colossian teachers did. Look at Jesus. Look at how He touched lepers, welcomed sinners, wept at a grave and forgave from the cross. That is the face of the invisible God."},{"title":"The One Holding Everything Together","verseRef":"v. 16-17","image":"Picture the night sky over the Lycus valley — the sky the Colossians looked at every night. The Milky Way stretching overhead, countless stars, the planets the pagans called gods. Many in Colossae believed these heavens were full of spiritual powers — thrones, dominions, rulers, authorities — who had to be appeased. Now zoom out further, past every star and galaxy, and zoom in further, past cells and atoms. At every scale, something holds it all together. Paul tells you His name.","meaning":"“For by him all things were created, in the heavens and on the earth, things visible and things invisible, whether thrones or dominions or principalities or powers; all things have been created through him, and for him. He is before all things, and in him all things are held together” (vv. 16–17). Christ is not one power among many; He made every power. He is not part of the universe; He sustains it. That means two things for you. First, there is no spiritual power you need to fear or court in addition to Christ; they were all made by Him and for Him. Second, if He holds galaxies and atoms together, He is able to hold your life together, even when it feels like it is coming apart."},{"title":"Moved to a New Country","verseRef":"v. 12-14","image":"In the ancient world, conquering kings sometimes moved entire populations — whole towns uprooted and resettled in another country. The word for this was μεθίστημι, to transfer. Now picture a different kind of transfer. A people is held captive in a dark land under a cruel ruler. A rescuer breaks in, drags them out of captivity, and carries them across the border into a bright kingdom, where they are given citizenship, land and a share in the King's inheritance. They will never go back.","meaning":"“who delivered us out of the power of darkness, and translated us into the Kingdom of the Son of his love; in whom we have our redemption, the forgiveness of our sins” (vv. 13–14). Every believer has been rescued and relocated. You no longer live under the authority of darkness. And the Father did not make you qualify for your new citizenship by rules, experiences or rituals: He “made us fit to be partakers of the inheritance of the saints in light” (v. 12). Redemption and forgiveness are already yours, in the present tense. When someone tells you that you need something more to really belong, remember where you live now, and who moved you there."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Greeting (v. 1-2)","body":"Paul, an apostle by God's will, and Timothy greet the faithful saints at Colossae."},{"label":"Thanksgiving for Faith, Love and Hope (v. 3-8)","body":"Paul thanks God for their faith and love, which spring from the hope stored up in heaven; the gospel they learned from Epaphras is the true gospel, bearing fruit and growing everywhere."},{"label":"Prayer for Knowledge, Fruit and Power (v. 9-14)","body":"He prays that they may be filled with the knowledge of God's will, live worthily, bear fruit, be strengthened for endurance with joy, and give thanks to the Father who qualified them, rescued them from darkness and transferred them into the kingdom of His Son, in whom they have redemption."},{"label":"The Hymn to Christ (v. 15-20)","body":"Christ is the image of the invisible God and firstborn over creation; all things were created in, through and for Him, and hold together in Him; He is head of the church, firstborn from the dead, preeminent in everything; all fullness dwells in Him, and through His cross God reconciles all things."},{"label":"Reconciled and Called to Continue (v. 21-23)","body":"Once alienated enemies, they are now reconciled through Christ's physical death to be presented holy and blameless, if they continue in the faith, not moved from the gospel's hope."},{"label":"Paul's Ministry of the Mystery (v. 24-29)","body":"Paul rejoices to suffer for the church, serving the mystery now revealed: Christ in you, the hope of glory; he proclaims Him to every person, labouring by Christ's power, to present everyone mature in Christ."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Faith, Love, Hope — and a Gospel That Needs No Supplement","paragraphs":["Paul opens Colossians as he opens most of his letters, with thanksgiving and prayer, but every line is chosen with the situation in Colossae in mind. He thanks God for their faith in Christ and their love for all the saints, “because of the hope which is laid up for you in the heavens” (vv. 4–5). The famous triad of faith, hope and love appears here, and hope is the root of the other two. Because their future was secure — ἀπόκειμαι, stored away, reserved — they were free to trust God and love others now. The false teachers were trying to make them anxious about whether they had done enough to reach spiritual fullness. Paul starts by reminding them that their inheritance is already reserved.","Then he affirms the gospel they first received. It came through “the word of the truth of the Good News,” it is “bearing fruit and growing” throughout the world, and they learned it from Epaphras, “a faithful servant of Christ on our behalf” (vv. 5–7). This matters. Paul has never visited Colossae, and the new teachers may have been suggesting that Epaphras had given them only an elementary version of the faith. Paul says, in effect: what Epaphras taught you is the real thing, the same gospel that is transforming people across the world. You do not need a more advanced gospel. You need to go deeper into the one you have.","Paul's prayer in verses 9 to 14 answers the false teaching point by point. They promised knowledge and fullness; Paul prays that the Colossians will be “filled with the knowledge of his will in all spiritual wisdom and understanding” (v. 9) — real fullness, from God. They promised a higher spiritual status; Paul prays for a worthy walk, fruitful work and growing knowledge of God (v. 10). They promised spiritual power; Paul prays for power “for all endurance and perseverance with joy” (v. 11) — power to keep going, gladly. And they implied the Colossians were not yet qualified; Paul gives thanks to the Father, “who made us fit to be partakers of the inheritance of the saints in light; who delivered us out of the power of darkness, and translated us into the Kingdom of the Son of his love; in whom we have our redemption, the forgiveness of our sins” (vv. 12–14). Every word is past or present tense. It is done. You are already qualified, rescued, transferred, redeemed and forgiven."]},{"heading":"The Hymn of the Supreme Christ","paragraphs":["Verses 15 to 20 are one of the most exalted passages about Christ in the entire Bible, and many scholars believe, as with Philippians 2, that Paul is using or adapting an early Christian hymn. It has two stanzas. The first (vv. 15–17) celebrates Christ as Lord of creation; the second (vv. 18–20) celebrates Him as Lord of the new creation, the church. Both begin with the same word: He is the πρωτότοκος, the firstborn — “of all creation” (v. 15) and “from the dead” (v. 18). The parallel is deliberate. The One who made the first creation is the One who begins the new one.","“Who is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of all creation” (v. 15). The word “firstborn” has sometimes been misread to mean that Christ was the first being God created. But that cannot be Paul's meaning, because the very next verse says “by him all things were created” — and if He created all things, He is not one of them. In the Old Testament, “firstborn” is a title of rank and inheritance. God says of David's royal line, “I will also appoint him my firstborn, the highest of the kings of the earth” (Psalm 89:27), though David was the youngest of his family. Christ is the firstborn in the sense of holding the supreme rank and the full inheritance over all creation.","And Paul makes Christ's supremacy over the spiritual powers explicit: “whether thrones or dominions or principalities or powers; all things have been created through him, and for him” (v. 16). This is aimed directly at the Colossian teaching, which seems to have encouraged reverence or worship of angelic powers (2:18). Paul says those powers are creatures, made by Christ and for Christ. Worshipping them instead of Him is like honouring the servants and ignoring the King. Then: “He is before all things, and in him all things are held together” (v. 17); “He is the head of the body, the assembly… that in all things he might have the preeminence” (v. 18); “For all the fullness was pleased to dwell in him; and through him to reconcile all things to himself… having made peace through the blood of his cross” (vv. 19–20). Creator, Sustainer, Head, Firstborn from the dead, dwelling place of all the fullness of God, Reconciler of all things through the cross. There is nothing left over for anyone else."]},{"heading":"Christ in You, the Hope of Glory — and the Afflictions Paul Fills Up","paragraphs":["From the cosmic scale Paul turns abruptly to the personal: “You, being in past times alienated and enemies in your mind in your evil deeds, yet now he has reconciled in the body of his flesh through death” (vv. 21–22). The reconciliation of all things in verse 20 includes the reconciliation of individual rebels. Notice the emphasis on Christ's physical body — “the body of his flesh” — which may answer teachers who devalued the physical or treated salvation as a purely spiritual ascent. Christ reconciled you in a real body, through a real death, so that you could be presented “holy and without defect and blameless before him.” And the condition in verse 23 — “if it is so that you continue in the faith, grounded and steadfast, and not moved away from the hope of the Good News” — is not a threat to true believers but a call to stay on the foundation. Genuine faith perseveres.","Verse 24 is one of the most debated sentences Paul wrote: “Now I rejoice in my sufferings for your sake, and fill up on my part that which is lacking of the afflictions of Christ in my flesh for his body’s sake, which is the assembly.” It cannot mean that Christ's atoning work was incomplete; the whole letter insists it is finished (1:20; 2:13–15). The word θλῖψις, affliction, is never used in the New Testament for Christ's redemptive suffering. The best reading is that Christ's body, the church, must still endure a measure of affliction as the gospel spreads, and that Paul gladly takes on more than his share so that others will be spared. Christ suffers in His people (as He told Saul on the Damascus road, “why do you persecute me?”), and Paul willingly absorbs those sufferings for the church's sake.","Then Paul reveals the content of the “mystery” he was commissioned to proclaim: “the mystery which has been hidden for ages and generations. But now it has been revealed to his saints, to whom God was pleased to make known what are the riches of the glory of this mystery among the Gentiles, which is Christ in you, the hope of glory” (vv. 26–27). The false teachers may have offered secret knowledge to a spiritual elite. Paul's mystery is no longer secret, and it is for everyone: the Messiah of Israel living in Gentile believers by His Spirit. And that is why Paul's ministry is so emphatically universal: “whom we proclaim, admonishing every man and teaching every man in all wisdom, that we may present every man perfect in Christ Jesus” (v. 28). Three times, “every man.” There is no inner circle in Christ. Every believer has Christ, and every believer can be brought to maturity in Him."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Testament Connection","paragraphs":["The description of Christ as “the image of the invisible God” (v. 15) is echoed across the New Testament. Paul writes in 2 Corinthians of “the light of the Good News of the glory of Christ, who is the image of God,” and says God “has shone in our hearts, to give the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ” (2 Corinthians 4:4, 6). John writes, “No one has seen God at any time. The one and only Son, who is in the bosom of the Father, he has declared him” (John 1:18). And Jesus Himself told Philip, “He who has seen me has seen the Father” (John 14:9). Hebrews says the Son is “the radiance of his glory, the very image of his substance, and upholding all things by the word of his power” (Hebrews 1:3) — the same truth as Colossians 1:17, “in him all things are held together.”","Christ's role in creation (v. 16) is stated in John's prologue: “All things were made through him. Without him was not anything made that has been made” (John 1:3). And His title “firstborn from the dead” (v. 18) appears in Revelation: “Jesus Christ, the faithful witness, the firstborn of the dead, and the ruler of the kings of the earth” (Revelation 1:5). Paul uses the same idea in 1 Corinthians: “But now Christ has been raised from the dead. He became the first fruits of those who are asleep” (1 Corinthians 15:20), and in Romans: God predestined believers “to be conformed to the image of his Son, that he might be the firstborn among many brothers” (Romans 8:29).","The rescue from darkness in verse 13 is the commission Paul received from the risen Christ on the Damascus road: “to open their eyes, that they may turn from darkness to light and from the power of Satan to God, that they may receive remission of sins and an inheritance among those who are sanctified by faith in me” (Acts 26:18). Notice the matching vocabulary: darkness to light, power, forgiveness, inheritance. Colossians 1:12–14 is that commission fulfilled in a church Paul had never visited. Peter describes the same transfer: God “called you out of darkness into his marvelous light” (1 Peter 2:9). And the reconciliation of verses 20 to 22 is the same message Paul preached to the Corinthians: “God was in Christ reconciling the world to himself, not reckoning to them their trespasses” (2 Corinthians 5:19)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Image","hebrew":"εἰκών","translit":"eikṓn","strongs":"G1504","verses":"v. 15 (and 3:10)","insight":"Strong's: “a likeness, i.e. (literally) statue, profile, or (figuratively) representation, resemblance.” Εἰκών was used of a portrait, a statue, or the emperor's image stamped on a coin (Matthew 22:20). In Genesis 1:27 humanity was made in God's image, and that image was marred by sin. Christ is the perfect image of the invisible God — not a copy that resembles God from the outside, but God Himself made visible. And in 3:10 Paul says believers are being “renewed in knowledge after the image of his Creator.” The image lost in Adam is restored in Christ. To look at Jesus is to see what God is like, and to see what we were made to be."},{"english":"Firstborn","hebrew":"πρωτότοκος","translit":"prōtótokos","strongs":"G4416","verses":"v. 15, 18","insight":"Strong's: “first-born (usually as noun, literally or figuratively).” In ordinary usage it meant the first child born. But in the Old Testament it became a title of rank, privilege and inheritance, not necessarily of birth order: Israel is God's “firstborn” among the nations (Exodus 4:22), and God says of the Davidic king, “I will also appoint him my firstborn, the highest of the kings of the earth” (Psalm 89:27). Christ is “firstborn of all creation” in the sense of supremacy and heirship over it — as verse 16 explains, He created it. And He is “firstborn from the dead” as the first to rise to resurrection life, guaranteeing that His brothers and sisters will follow (Romans 8:29)."},{"english":"Hold together / consist","hebrew":"συνιστάω","translit":"synistáō","strongs":"G4921","verses":"v. 17","insight":"Strong's: “to set together, i.e. (by implication) to introduce (favorably), or (figuratively) to exhibit; intransitively, to stand near, or (figuratively) to constitute.” The perfect tense in verse 17 means “stand together, cohere, are held in place.” Greek philosophers asked what principle held the universe together; some answered the Logos, the rational order of the world. Paul names a Person. Christ is not only the agent of creation in the past; He is the One who sustains it continuously. Hebrews 1:3 says the same: He is “upholding all things by the word of his power.” If He stopped, nothing would stay together. That is the Christ who holds your life."},{"english":"Fullness","hebrew":"πλήρωμα","translit":"plḗrōma","strongs":"G4138","verses":"v. 19 (and 2:9)","insight":"Strong's: “repletion or completion, i.e. (subjectively) what fills (as contents, supplement, copiousness, multitude), or (objectively) what is filled.” Πλήρωμα appears to have been a significant word in the teaching threatening Colossae, and in later Gnostic systems it described the totality of divine beings and emanations distributed between God and the world. Paul's answer is decisive: “all the fullness” dwells in Christ (1:19), and “in him all the fullness of the Deity dwells bodily” (2:9). The fullness is not scattered among angels or levels of spiritual beings; it is concentrated entirely in one Person. And 2:10 adds: in Him you are made full. You need no other source."},{"english":"Reconcile fully","hebrew":"ἀποκαταλλάσσω","translit":"apokatallássō","strongs":"G604","verses":"v. 20, 22 (and Ephesians 2:16)","insight":"Strong's: “to reconcile fully.” This is an intensified form of καταλλάσσω, the usual word for reconciliation, with the prefix ἀπό adding the sense of a complete restoration. It appears only in Colossians 1:20, 22 and Ephesians 2:16, all three times for what Christ did on the cross. Reconciliation in Scripture means the removal of hostility and the restoration of relationship. And verse 20 shows its scope: God reconciles “all things… whether things on the earth, or things in the heavens.” This does not mean everyone will be saved regardless of faith (v. 23 requires continuing in the faith). It means the cross is the means by which God restores His whole creation to order under Christ."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the size of your Christ, the qualifications you think you still need, and the endurance you have been asking God to remove instead of to supply.","items":[{"title":"How Big Is Your Christ?","body":"Many believers relate to Jesus as a helper for personal problems, a friend, a teacher — all true. But Colossians 1 shows Him as the Creator of galaxies and angels, the One in whom every atom holds together, the head of the church, the firstborn from the dead, the One in whom all the fullness of God dwells. The Spirit wants to enlarge your vision. Your anxieties, your fears, your sense that life is coming apart, your temptation to look for help elsewhere — all of them shrink when you see how great Christ is. Read verses 15 to 20 aloud slowly today, and after each line, say: “And He is my Lord.”"},{"title":"You Are Already Qualified","body":"Some voice — perhaps a teacher, a church culture, or your own conscience — has been telling you that you are not quite a real Christian yet. That you need a certain experience, a higher level of discipline, a deeper revelation, a more impressive spiritual life. The Spirit wants you to hear verse 12: the Father “made us fit to be partakers of the inheritance of the saints in light.” You did not qualify yourself, and no one can disqualify you by adding requirements. You have been rescued from darkness, transferred into the kingdom of the Son, redeemed and forgiven. Grow, yes. But grow from security, not toward it."},{"title":"Power for Endurance","body":"You may have been asking God for power to change your circumstances: to remove a difficulty, to end a hard season, to fix a relationship. Paul prays for power too — “strengthened with all power, according to the might of his glory” — but look at what the power is for: “for all endurance and perseverance with joy” (v. 11). Sometimes God's greatest power is shown not in removing the trial but in enabling you to stay in it with joy. The Spirit may be answering your prayer in a different way than you expected. Ask Him today for the power to endure, patiently and gladly."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Preeminence Check","body":"Verse 18 says Christ must have “the preeminence” in all things. Write down the main areas of your life: family, work, money, time, relationships, entertainment, ambitions. Beside each one, ask honestly: is Christ first here, or is He one consideration among many? Circle the area where He is least preeminent."},{"title":"The Qualification Check","body":"Is there anything you feel you must achieve or experience before you can be confident of your standing with God? Write it down. Then read verses 12 to 14 aloud and ask: what does this passage say God has already done for me?"},{"title":"The Endurance Check","body":"What situation in your life right now requires endurance (circumstances) or patience (people)? Have you been praying mainly for it to end, or also for the power to endure it with joy? Write a new prayer for it based on verse 11."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Praying Colossians 1:9–12","body":"Choose three believers — one of them someone you have never met but have heard about, as Paul had heard about the Colossians. Every day this week, pray Paul's prayer for each by name: that they be filled with the knowledge of God's will in all wisdom and understanding; that they walk worthily of the Lord, bear fruit in every good work and grow in knowing God; that they be strengthened with all power for endurance and patience with joy; and that they give thanks to the Father who qualified them. At the end of the week, if possible, tell one of them how you have been praying."},{"title":"Worshipping the Supreme Christ","body":"Memorise Colossians 1:15–18 this week. Each evening, go outside if you can and look at the sky — the stars, the clouds, the vastness — and recite the passage aloud. Then bring your own needs and fears into that vision: “Lord, You hold all things together. You hold my family, my health, my future, my fears.” Notice how your worries change size when you have looked at Christ first."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, I give You thanks. You have made me fit to share in the inheritance of the saints in light. You rescued me from the domain of darkness and transferred me into the kingdom of the Son You love, in whom I have redemption, the forgiveness of my sins. I am not trying to qualify; You have qualified me. Lord Jesus, You are the image of the invisible God, the firstborn over all creation. By You all things were created — in heaven and on earth, visible and invisible, thrones and dominions, rulers and authorities. All things were created through You and for You. You are before all things, and in You all things hold together. You are the head of the body, the church, the beginning, the firstborn from the dead. Be preeminent in everything in my life. All the fullness of God dwells in You, and through Your blood shed on the cross You have made peace and reconciled me to God. I was once an enemy in my mind; now I am reconciled to be presented holy and blameless. Keep me grounded and steadfast, not moved from the hope of the gospel. Fill me with the knowledge of Your will in all wisdom and spiritual understanding. Strengthen me with all power for endurance and patience with joy. And thank You for the mystery revealed: Christ in me, the hope of glory. In His name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"In the Beginning","ref":"Genesis 1:1-31; John 1:1-3","relatedVerses":"v. 15-17","summary":"The Bible opens with God speaking the universe into existence: light, sky, sea, land, plants, stars, living creatures, and finally humanity, made in His image. Each day God speaks, and what He speaks comes to be. John's Gospel tells us who that Word was: “In the beginning was the Word… All things were made through him.” Colossians 1 tells us the same: “by him all things were created.” When you read Genesis 1, you are watching the Son at work. And the image of God given to humanity on the sixth day — marred in the fall — is restored in the One who is Himself “the image of the invisible God.” Creation and redemption have the same Author."},{"title":"From Darkness to Light on the Damascus Road","ref":"Acts 9:1-19; 26:12-18","relatedVerses":"v. 12-14","summary":"Saul was travelling to Damascus with authority to arrest Christians when a light from heaven, brighter than the sun at noon, flashed around him. He fell to the ground and heard the voice of the risen Jesus. He was blind for three days, and then a disciple named Ananias laid hands on him, something like scales fell from his eyes, and he was baptised. Later Paul told King Agrippa what Jesus had commissioned him to do: open people's eyes, turn them from darkness to light and from the power of Satan to God, so that they would receive forgiveness of sins and an inheritance. Paul had himself been rescued from the domain of darkness and transferred into the kingdom of the Son. Colossians 1:12–14 is his own testimony, written to Gentiles who had experienced the same rescue."},{"title":"The Glory Fills the Temple","ref":"1 Kings 8:1-11","relatedVerses":"v. 19","summary":"When Solomon finished building the temple, the priests brought the ark of the covenant into the Most Holy Place. As they came out, a cloud filled the house of the LORD, so that the priests could not stand to minister, because the glory of the LORD filled the temple. God had chosen to make His dwelling among His people in a house built with hands. Colossians 1:19 says something far greater: “all the fullness was pleased to dwell in him.” The glory that once filled a building now dwells permanently in a Person. And because Christ lives in His people, the glory that filled Solomon's temple now dwells in believers too: “Christ in you, the hope of glory.”"}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Colossians","chapter":2,"chapterTitle":"Complete in Him — The Cross That Cancelled the Debt and Disarmed the Powers","themeTitle":"“Why Go Back to the Shadow When You Have the Substance?”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"For I desire to have you know how greatly I struggle for you, and for those at Laodicea, and for as many as have not seen my face in the flesh;","understanding":"Paul wants them to know how great an ἀγών — struggle, contest, agony — he has for them, and for those at Λαοδίκεια, and for all who have never seen his face. His struggle is mostly in prayer (compare 4:12, where Epaphras is “always striving for you in his prayers”). Paul is fighting for a church he has never visited.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G73","hebrew":"ἀγών","translit":"agṓn","english":"struggle"},{"strongs":"G2993","hebrew":"Λαοδίκεια","translit":"Laodíkeia","english":"Laodicea"},{"strongs":"G4383","hebrew":"πρόσωπον","translit":"prósōpon","english":"face"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"}]},{"v":2,"text":"that their hearts may be comforted, they being knit together in love, and gaining all riches of the full assurance of understanding, that they may know the mystery of God, both of the Father and of Christ,","understanding":"His goal: that their hearts may be παρακαλέω — encouraged, strengthened — συμβιβασθέντες ἐν ἀγάπῃ, knit together in love, and reach all the riches of πληροφορία — full assurance, complete confidence — of understanding, so that they may know God's μυστήριον: Christ Himself. Love and understanding grow together. A church knit in love is hard to deceive.","words":[{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"comforted"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"hearts"},{"strongs":"G4822","hebrew":"συμβιβάζω","translit":"symbibázō","english":"knit together"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G4149","hebrew":"πλοῦτος","translit":"ploûtos","english":"riches"},{"strongs":"G4136","hebrew":"πληροφορία","translit":"plērophoría","english":"full assurance"},{"strongs":"G4907","hebrew":"σύνεσις","translit":"sýnesis","english":"understanding"},{"strongs":"G1922","hebrew":"ἐπίγνωσις","translit":"epígnōsis","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G3466","hebrew":"μυστήριον","translit":"mystḗrion","english":"mystery"}]},{"v":3,"text":"in whom are all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge hidden.","understanding":"Ἐν ᾧ εἰσιν πάντες οἱ θησαυροὶ τῆς σοφίας καὶ γνώσεως ἀπόκρυφοι — in whom are all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge, hidden. The false teachers promised hidden treasures of secret wisdom to the initiated. Paul uses their vocabulary: yes, there are hidden treasures — and they are all in Christ. You do not need to look anywhere else.","words":[{"strongs":"G2344","hebrew":"θησαυρός","translit":"thēsaurós","english":"treasures"},{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G1108","hebrew":"γνῶσις","translit":"gnōsis","english":"knowledge"},{"strongs":"G614","hebrew":"ἀπόκρυφος","translit":"apókryphos","english":"hidden"}]},{"v":4,"text":"Now this I say that no one may delude you with persuasiveness of speech.","understanding":"Paul says this so that no one may παραλογίζομαι — mislead, reason you into error with false arguments — ἐν πιθανολογίᾳ, with plausible, persuasive speech. Error rarely arrives sounding foolish. It usually sounds convincing.","words":[{"strongs":"G3884","hebrew":"παραλογίζομαι","translit":"paralogízomai","english":"delude"},{"strongs":"G4086","hebrew":"πιθανολογία","translit":"pithanología","english":"persuasiveness of speech"}]},{"v":5,"text":"For though I am absent in the flesh, yet am I with you in the spirit, rejoicing and seeing your order, and the steadfastness of your faith in Christ.","understanding":"Though absent in body, Paul is with them in spirit, rejoicing to see their τάξις — good order, like soldiers in formation — and the στερέωμα, the firmness, solidity, of their faith in Christ. Two military words. The church is still standing in line; Paul wants it to stay that way.","words":[{"strongs":"G548","hebrew":"ἄπειμι","translit":"ápeimi","english":"absent"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirit"},{"strongs":"G5463","hebrew":"χαίρω","translit":"chaírō","english":"rejoicing"},{"strongs":"G5010","hebrew":"τάξις","translit":"táxis","english":"order"},{"strongs":"G4733","hebrew":"στερέωμα","translit":"steréōma","english":"steadfastness"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"}]},{"v":6,"text":"As therefore you received Christ Jesus, the Lord, walk in him,","understanding":"The key verse of the letter's practical argument. Ὡς οὖν παρελάβετε τὸν Χριστὸν Ἰησοῦν τὸν κύριον — as you received Christ Jesus the Lord — ἐν αὐτῷ περιπατεῖτε, in Him walk. You did not receive Christ by faith and then grow by rules or mystical experiences. You continue the way you began: in Christ, by faith.","words":[{"strongs":"G3880","hebrew":"παραλαμβάνω","translit":"paralambánō","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G4043","hebrew":"περιπατέω","translit":"peripatéō","english":"walk"}]},{"v":7,"text":"rooted and built up in him, and established in the faith, even as you were taught, abounding in it in thanksgiving.","understanding":"Four pictures in a row. Ἐρριζωμένοι — rooted, like a tree (perfect tense: once rooted, still rooted). Ἐποικοδομούμενοι — being built up, like a building (present: still being built). Βεβαιούμενοι — established, confirmed, a legal word for a guaranteed contract. And περισσεύοντες ἐν εὐχαριστίᾳ — overflowing with thanksgiving. Thankfulness is the natural sound of a person who knows they already have everything in Christ.","words":[{"strongs":"G4492","hebrew":"ῥιζόω","translit":"rhizóō","english":"rooted"},{"strongs":"G2026","hebrew":"ἐποικοδομέω","translit":"epoikodoméō","english":"built up"},{"strongs":"G950","hebrew":"βεβαιόω","translit":"bebaióō","english":"established"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G1321","hebrew":"διδάσκω","translit":"didáskō","english":"taught"},{"strongs":"G4052","hebrew":"περισσεύω","translit":"perisseúō","english":"abounding"},{"strongs":"G2169","hebrew":"εὐχαριστία","translit":"eucharistía","english":"thanksgiving"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Be careful that you don’t let anyone rob you through his philosophy and vain deceit, after the tradition of men, after the elements of the world, and not after Christ.","understanding":"Βλέπετε — watch out — that no one συλαγωγέω — carries you off as plunder, kidnaps you as a captive. This rare word, found only here in the New Testament, pictures a raider dragging away prisoners. The weapons: φιλοσοφία καὶ κενῆς ἀπάτης — philosophy, that is, empty deceit — according to the παράδοσις, the tradition, of men, according to the στοιχεῖα τοῦ κόσμου — the elemental things of the world (either basic religious principles, or elemental spiritual powers; both meanings are possible) — and not according to Christ.","words":[{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"be careful"},{"strongs":"G4812","hebrew":"συλαγωγέω","translit":"sylagōgéō","english":"rob"},{"strongs":"G5385","hebrew":"φιλοσοφία","translit":"philosophía","english":"philosophy"},{"strongs":"G2756","hebrew":"κενός","translit":"kenós","english":"vain"},{"strongs":"G539","hebrew":"ἀπάτη","translit":"apátē","english":"deceit"},{"strongs":"G3862","hebrew":"παράδοσις","translit":"parádosis","english":"tradition"},{"strongs":"G4747","hebrew":"στοιχεῖον","translit":"stoicheîon","english":"elements"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"}]},{"v":9,"text":"For in him all the fullness of the Deity dwells bodily,","understanding":"The reason: ὅτι ἐν αὐτῷ κατοικεῖ πᾶν τὸ πλήρωμα τῆς θεότητος σωματικῶς. In Him dwells — permanently, settled at home — all the fullness of θεότης, deity, the very nature of God, not merely divine qualities — σωματικῶς, bodily. God's entire being lives in a human body in Christ. This is one of the clearest statements of Christ's full deity and full humanity in the New Testament.","words":[{"strongs":"G2730","hebrew":"κατοικέω","translit":"katoikéō","english":"dwells"},{"strongs":"G4138","hebrew":"πλήρωμα","translit":"plḗrōma","english":"fullness"},{"strongs":"G2320","hebrew":"θεότης","translit":"theótēs","english":"Deity"},{"strongs":"G4985","hebrew":"σωματικῶς","translit":"sōmatikōs","english":"bodily"}]},{"v":10,"text":"and in him you are made full, who is the head of all principality and power;","understanding":"Καὶ ἐστὲ ἐν αὐτῷ πεπληρωμένοι — and you are in Him having been filled, made complete (perfect tense: filled and still full). He is ἡ κεφαλὴ πάσης ἀρχῆς καὶ ἐξουσίας — the head over every ruler and authority. The powers the false teachers wanted to appease are under Christ. And you are in Christ. You do not need to climb up to them; you are already seated above them.","words":[{"strongs":"G4137","hebrew":"πληρόω","translit":"plēróō","english":"made full"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"head"},{"strongs":"G746","hebrew":"ἀρχή","translit":"archḗ","english":"principality"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"power"}]},{"v":11,"text":"in whom you were also circumcised with a circumcision not made with hands, in the putting off of the body of the sins of the flesh, in the circumcision of Christ;","understanding":"In Him you were circumcised with a περιτομή ἀχειροποιήτῳ — a circumcision not made with hands — in the ἀπέκδυσις, the stripping off, of the body of the flesh, in the circumcision of Christ. Some false teachers insisted on physical circumcision. Paul says believers already have the real thing: a spiritual cutting away of the old sinful self. Whether “the circumcision of Christ” means the circumcision Christ performs in us, or Christ's own death (His body stripped off on the cross), the effect is the same: the old life has been cut away.","words":[{"strongs":"G4059","hebrew":"περιτέμνω","translit":"peritémnō","english":"circumcised"},{"strongs":"G4061","hebrew":"περιτομή","translit":"peritomḗ","english":"circumcision"},{"strongs":"G886","hebrew":"ἀχειροποίητος","translit":"acheiropoíētos","english":"not made with hands"},{"strongs":"G555","hebrew":"ἀπέκδυσις","translit":"apékdysis","english":"putting off"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"}]},{"v":12,"text":"having been buried with him in baptism, in which you were also raised with him through faith in the working of God, who raised him from the dead.","understanding":"Συνταφέντες — buried together — with Him in βάπτισμα, baptism, in which you were also συνηγέρθητε — raised together — with Him through faith in the ἐνέργεια, the working, of God, who raised Him from the dead. Baptism is the picture: going down into the water is burial, coming up is resurrection. And the power is not in the water but in faith in God's working.","words":[{"strongs":"G4916","hebrew":"συνθάπτω","translit":"syntháptō","english":"buried with"},{"strongs":"G908","hebrew":"βάπτισμα","translit":"báptisma","english":"baptism"},{"strongs":"G4891","hebrew":"συνεγείρω","translit":"synegeírō","english":"raised with"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G1753","hebrew":"ἐνέργεια","translit":"enérgeia","english":"working"},{"strongs":"G1453","hebrew":"ἐγείρω","translit":"egeírō","english":"raised"}]},{"v":13,"text":"You were dead through your trespasses and the uncircumcision of your flesh. He made you alive together with him, having forgiven us all our trespasses,","understanding":"Once you were νεκροί, dead, in your παραπτώματα (trespasses, false steps) and the uncircumcision of your flesh — spiritually lifeless and outside the covenant. He συνεζωοποίησεν — made alive together — you with Him, χαρισάμενος, having freely forgiven, us all our trespasses. Notice Paul shifts from “you” to “us.” He needed the same forgiveness.","words":[{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G3900","hebrew":"παράπτωμα","translit":"paráptōma","english":"trespasses"},{"strongs":"G203","hebrew":"ἀκροβυστία","translit":"akrobystía","english":"uncircumcision"},{"strongs":"G4806","hebrew":"συζωοποιέω","translit":"syzōopoiéō","english":"made alive together"},{"strongs":"G5483","hebrew":"χαρίζομαι","translit":"charízomai","english":"forgiven"}]},{"v":14,"text":"wiping out the handwriting in ordinances which was against us; and he has taken it out of the way, nailing it to the cross;","understanding":"Ἐξαλείψας — wiping out, erasing, as ink was washed off papyrus — τὸ καθ’ ἡμῶν χειρόγραφον, the handwritten document against us. A χειρόγραφον was a signed certificate of debt, an IOU in the debtor's own handwriting. Τοῖς δόγμασιν — with its regulations, decrees — which was ὑπεναντίον, hostile, to us. And He ἦρκεν — has taken it away (perfect tense: removed permanently) — προσηλώσας αὐτὸ τῷ σταυρῷ, nailing it to the cross. Your debt was nailed up with Christ and cancelled.","words":[{"strongs":"G1813","hebrew":"ἐξαλείφω","translit":"exaleíphō","english":"wiping out"},{"strongs":"G5498","hebrew":"χειρόγραφον","translit":"cheirógraphon","english":"handwriting"},{"strongs":"G1378","hebrew":"δόγμα","translit":"dógma","english":"ordinances"},{"strongs":"G5227","hebrew":"ὑπεναντίος","translit":"hypenantíos","english":"against us"},{"strongs":"G142","hebrew":"αἴρω","translit":"aírō","english":"taken out of the way"},{"strongs":"G4338","hebrew":"προσηλόω","translit":"prosēlóō","english":"nailing"},{"strongs":"G4716","hebrew":"σταυρός","translit":"staurós","english":"cross"}]},{"v":15,"text":"having stripped the principalities and the powers, he made a show of them openly, triumphing over them in it.","understanding":"Ἀπεκδυσάμενος — having stripped, disarmed — the ἀρχαί and ἐξουσίαι, the rulers and authorities, ἐδειγμάτισεν ἐν παρρησίᾳ — He made a public spectacle of them, exposed them openly — θριαμβεύσας αὐτοὺς ἐν αὐτῷ, triumphing over them in it. Θριαμβεύω means to lead in a triumphal procession: the conquering Roman general paraded his captured enemies through the streets. The cross looked like Christ's defeat. It was the powers' defeat.","words":[{"strongs":"G554","hebrew":"ἀπεκδύομαι","translit":"apekdýomai","english":"stripped"},{"strongs":"G746","hebrew":"ἀρχή","translit":"archḗ","english":"principalities"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"powers"},{"strongs":"G1165","hebrew":"δειγματίζω","translit":"deigmatízō","english":"made a show of"},{"strongs":"G3954","hebrew":"παῤῥησία","translit":"parrhēsía","english":"openly"},{"strongs":"G2358","hebrew":"θριαμβεύω","translit":"thriambeúō","english":"triumphing"}]},{"v":16,"text":"Let no one therefore judge you in eating, or in drinking, or with respect to a feast day or a new moon or a Sabbath day,","understanding":"Μὴ οὖν τις ὑμᾶς κρινέτω — therefore let no one judge you — in βρῶσις (eating) or πόσις (drinking), or regarding an ἑορτή (festival), a νουμηνία (new moon) or σάββατα (Sabbaths). These were Jewish calendar observances; the false teachers apparently made them a test of spirituality. Paul does not say the practices are evil. He says no one may make them a requirement or a basis to judge you.","words":[{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judge"},{"strongs":"G1035","hebrew":"βρῶσις","translit":"brōsis","english":"eating"},{"strongs":"G4213","hebrew":"πόσις","translit":"pósis","english":"drinking"},{"strongs":"G1859","hebrew":"ἑορτή","translit":"heortḗ","english":"feast day"},{"strongs":"G3561","hebrew":"νουμηνία","translit":"noumēnía","english":"new moon"},{"strongs":"G4521","hebrew":"σάββατον","translit":"sábbaton","english":"Sabbath day"}]},{"v":17,"text":"which are a shadow of the things to come; but the body is Christ’s.","understanding":"Ἅ ἐστιν σκιὰ τῶν μελλόντων — which are a shadow of the things to come — τὸ δὲ σῶμα τοῦ Χριστοῦ, but the body, the substance, the reality, belongs to Christ. A shadow shows the shape of something real. When the real thing arrives, you do not keep hugging its shadow. The festivals and food laws pointed to Christ. He has come.","words":[{"strongs":"G4639","hebrew":"σκιά","translit":"skiá","english":"shadow"},{"strongs":"G3195","hebrew":"μέλλω","translit":"méllō","english":"to come"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Let no one rob you of your prize by a voluntary humility and worshiping of the angels, dwelling in the things which he has not seen, vainly puffed up by his fleshly mind,","understanding":"Μηδεὶς ὑμᾶς καταβραβευέτω — let no one disqualify you, act as umpire against you, rob you of the prize — by insisting on ταπεινοφροσύνη (a show of humility; probably ascetic practices) and the θρησκεία τῶν ἀγγέλων (worship of angels), ἃ ἑόρακεν ἐμβατεύων — taking his stand on (or “entering into”) visions he has seen. Ἐμβατεύω was used in some ancient mystery religions for entering the sanctuary after initiation. Such a person is εἰκῇ φυσιούμενος — puffed up without reason — by his fleshly mind. Claims of special visions often produce pride, not humility.","words":[{"strongs":"G2603","hebrew":"καταβραβεύω","translit":"katabrabeúō","english":"rob of your prize"},{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"voluntary"},{"strongs":"G5012","hebrew":"ταπεινοφροσύνη","translit":"tapeinophrosýnē","english":"humility"},{"strongs":"G2356","hebrew":"θρησκεία","translit":"thrēskeía","english":"worshiping"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"seen"},{"strongs":"G1687","hebrew":"ἐμβατεύω","translit":"embateúō","english":"dwelling (entering)"},{"strongs":"G1500","hebrew":"εἰκῆ","translit":"eikē","english":"vainly"},{"strongs":"G5448","hebrew":"φυσιόω","translit":"physióō","english":"puffed up"},{"strongs":"G3563","hebrew":"νοῦς","translit":"noûs","english":"mind"}]},{"v":19,"text":"and not holding firmly to the Head, from whom all the body, being supplied and knit together through the joints and ligaments, grows with God’s growth.","understanding":"And οὐ κρατῶν τὴν κεφαλήν — not holding fast to the Head. That is the real problem. Their visions and practices had detached them from Christ. From the Head the whole body, supplied (ἐπιχορηγέω) and held together (συμβιβάζω) through its ἁφαί (joints) and σύνδεσμοι (ligaments), grows τὴν αὔξησιν τοῦ θεοῦ — with the growth that comes from God. Spiritual growth comes from connection to the Head, not from add-ons.","words":[{"strongs":"G2902","hebrew":"κρατέω","translit":"kratéō","english":"holding firmly"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"Head"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G2023","hebrew":"ἐπιχορηγέω","translit":"epichorēgéō","english":"supplied"},{"strongs":"G4822","hebrew":"συμβιβάζω","translit":"symbibázō","english":"knit together"},{"strongs":"G860","hebrew":"ἁφή","translit":"haphḗ","english":"joints"},{"strongs":"G4886","hebrew":"σύνδεσμος","translit":"sýndesmos","english":"ligaments"},{"strongs":"G837","hebrew":"αὐξάνω","translit":"auxánō","english":"grows"},{"strongs":"G838","hebrew":"αὔξησις","translit":"aúxēsis","english":"growth"}]},{"v":20,"text":"If you died with Christ from the elements of the world, why, as though living in the world, do you subject yourselves to ordinances,","understanding":"If you died with Christ ἀπὸ τῶν στοιχείων τοῦ κόσμου — away from the elemental things of the world — why, as if still living in the world, do you δογματίζω — submit to decrees, let yourselves be regulated? You are dead to that whole system. Why go back to living under it?","words":[{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"died"},{"strongs":"G4747","hebrew":"στοιχεῖον","translit":"stoicheîon","english":"elements"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G1379","hebrew":"δογματίζω","translit":"dogmatízō","english":"subject yourselves to ordinances"}]},{"v":21,"text":"“Don’t handle, nor taste, nor touch”","understanding":"Paul quotes their slogans with a touch of scorn: “Μὴ ἅψῃ μηδὲ γεύσῃ μηδὲ θίγῃς — Don't handle, don't taste, don't touch!” A religion defined by a list of things not to touch.","words":[{"strongs":"G680","hebrew":"ἅπτομαι","translit":"háptomai","english":"handle"},{"strongs":"G1089","hebrew":"γεύομαι","translit":"geúomai","english":"taste"},{"strongs":"G2345","hebrew":"θιγγάνω","translit":"thingánō","english":"touch"}]},{"v":22,"text":"(all of which perish with use), according to the precepts and doctrines of men?","understanding":"All these things are destined εἰς φθορὰν τῇ ἀποχρήσει — to perish as they are used. Food is eaten and gone. The rules concern things that do not last. And they are κατὰ τὰ ἐντάλματα καὶ διδασκαλίας τῶν ἀνθρώπων — according to human commands and teachings, an echo of Isaiah 29:13, which Jesus applied to the Pharisees' food rules (Mark 7:7).","words":[{"strongs":"G5356","hebrew":"φθορά","translit":"phthorá","english":"perish"},{"strongs":"G671","hebrew":"ἀπόχρησις","translit":"apóchrēsis","english":"use"},{"strongs":"G1778","hebrew":"ἔνταλμα","translit":"éntalma","english":"precepts"},{"strongs":"G1319","hebrew":"διδασκαλία","translit":"didaskalía","english":"doctrines"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"}]},{"v":23,"text":"Which things indeed appear like wisdom in self-imposed worship, and humility, and severity to the body; but aren’t of any value against the indulgence of the flesh.","understanding":"The final verdict. These things have λόγον σοφίας — a reputation for wisdom — ἐν ἐθελοθρησκίᾳ — in self-made religion, worship of one's own devising (a word perhaps coined by Paul) — ταπεινοφροσύνῃ (humility) and ἀφειδίᾳ σώματος (severe treatment of the body). They look impressive. But they are οὐκ ἐν τιμῇ τινι πρὸς πλησμονὴν τῆς σαρκός — of no value against the indulgence of the flesh. Rules can restrain behaviour for a while; they cannot change the heart. Only Christ can.","words":[{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"appear (reputation)"},{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G1479","hebrew":"ἐθελοθρησκεία","translit":"ethelothrēskeía","english":"self-imposed worship"},{"strongs":"G5012","hebrew":"ταπεινοφροσύνη","translit":"tapeinophrosýnē","english":"humility"},{"strongs":"G857","hebrew":"ἀφειδία","translit":"apheidía","english":"severity"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"value"},{"strongs":"G4140","hebrew":"πλησμονή","translit":"plēsmonḗ","english":"indulgence"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The IOU Nailed to the Cross","verseRef":"v. 13-14","image":"Picture a document from the ancient world: a χειρόγραφον, a certificate of debt, written in the debtor's own hand and signed. It lists what he owes, and it is held by the creditor as legal proof. Now picture that your own document exists — every command of God you broke, every debt you could never repay, written in your own handwriting, because you did these things yourself. It is held against you. Then picture a hand taking that document, washing the ink off the papyrus until the page is blank, and carrying it to a hill outside Jerusalem. A nail is driven through it into the wood of a cross. Above Jesus' head, the Romans nailed the charge against Him. Beneath it, God nailed the charge against you.","meaning":"“wiping out the handwriting in ordinances which was against us; and he has taken it out of the way, nailing it to the cross” (v. 14). Notice the verbs: wiped out, taken away (perfect tense — permanently), nailed. Your debt was not reduced or put on a payment plan. It was erased and cancelled at the cross. That is why Paul can say God “made you alive together with him, having forgiven us all our trespasses” (v. 13). The Colossian teachers wanted believers to keep paying through rules, festivals and harsh self-denial. But you cannot pay a debt that has already been nailed up and cancelled. Stop trying to pay it. Thank the One who did."},{"title":"The Triumph Through the Streets","verseRef":"v. 15","image":"A Roman triumph was the greatest spectacle in the empire. When a general won a great victory, he rode through Rome in a golden chariot, and in front of him marched his captives — enemy kings and generals, stripped of their armour and weapons, in chains, paraded before the jeering crowds. Everyone could see they were defeated. Now picture the cross. To the watching world it looked like Jesus was the captive: stripped, mocked, nailed up, dying. But Paul says the opposite was happening. On that cross, Christ “stripped the principalities and the powers” and “made a show of them openly, triumphing over them in it.”","meaning":"The Colossians were being told that spiritual powers had to be appeased, venerated or navigated through special rituals and knowledge. Paul's answer is that those powers were disarmed and paraded in defeat at the cross. Whatever accusations they could bring — and the greatest was the record of our sin — were cancelled in the previous verse. They have no legal claim left. Jesus said before His death, “Now the prince of this world will be cast out” (John 12:31), and John wrote, “To this end the Son of God was revealed: that he might destroy the works of the devil” (1 John 3:8). You do not need to fear or court the powers. You belong to the Victor."},{"title":"Hugging a Shadow","verseRef":"v. 16-17","image":"Picture a child waiting at the window for a father who has been away a long time. In the late afternoon sun, she sees a long shadow fall across the path to the door — the shape of a man. She runs outside and throws her arms around the shadow on the ground, hugging the dark shape on the stones. And her father, standing right there in the flesh, laughs and lifts her up. Why hold the shadow when the person is here?","meaning":"“Let no one therefore judge you in eating, or in drinking, or with respect to a feast day or a new moon or a Sabbath day, which are a shadow of the things to come; but the body is Christ’s” (vv. 16–17). The Old Testament festivals, food laws and holy days were good gifts from God — but they were shadows, shapes cast ahead of time by the coming Christ. Hebrews says the same: the law had “a shadow of the good to come, not the very image of the things” (Hebrews 10:1). When teachers insist on shadows as the measure of spirituality, they are asking you to hug the shape on the ground while the Person stands in front of you. Hold on to Christ, the substance."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Paul's Struggle for Them (v. 1-5)","body":"Paul labours in prayer for churches he has never visited, that they be encouraged, knit together in love and reach full understanding of Christ, in whom all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge are hidden — so that no one deceives them with plausible arguments."},{"label":"Walk in Christ as You Received Him (v. 6-7)","body":"As they received Christ Jesus the Lord, they must continue to live in Him, rooted, built up, established in the faith and overflowing with thanksgiving."},{"label":"Complete in Christ (v. 8-15)","body":"Beware of human philosophy and tradition; in Christ all the fullness of deity dwells bodily, and believers are complete in Him — spiritually circumcised, buried and raised with Him, made alive and forgiven; He cancelled the debt against us by nailing it to the cross and disarmed and triumphed over the powers."},{"label":"Do Not Return to Shadows (v. 16-19)","body":"Let no one judge you about food, drink, festivals or Sabbaths, which are shadows of Christ, the substance; let no one disqualify you by insisting on false humility, angel worship and visions, cut off from the Head from whom the whole body grows."},{"label":"Dead to Human Regulations (v. 20-23)","body":"Since they died with Christ to the elemental things of the world, they should not submit to rules like “don't handle, taste or touch,” which have an appearance of wisdom but no power against the flesh."}],"sections":[{"heading":"As You Received Him, So Walk in Him","paragraphs":["Colossians 2 is the polemical heart of the letter, where Paul confronts the false teaching directly. But he begins with love and prayer. He tells them of his ἀγών, his agonised struggle, for them and for the believers in Laodicea, “and for as many as have not seen my face in the flesh” (v. 1). His goal is that their hearts would be encouraged and “knit together in love,” and that they would have “full assurance of understanding, that they may know the mystery of God, both of the Father and of Christ, in whom are all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge hidden” (vv. 2–3). The false teachers offered hidden treasures of special knowledge. Paul uses the very word, ἀπόκρυφοι, hidden, and says all such treasures are already hidden in Christ. You do not need to go anywhere else to find them.","Then comes the turning point of the letter: “As therefore you received Christ Jesus, the Lord, walk in him, rooted and built up in him, and established in the faith, even as you were taught, abounding in it in thanksgiving” (vv. 6–7). This is a principle that guards against nearly every form of false spirituality. The Colossians had received Christ by faith, through the gospel Epaphras preached. Now someone was telling them that to grow they needed something else — rules, visions, rituals, a higher level of insight. Paul says: you grow the same way you began. You received Christ; walk in Christ. The Christian life is not “Christ plus.” It is Christ, more deeply.","Paul piles up images in verse 7: rooted like a tree, built up like a house, established like a guaranteed legal contract, and overflowing with thanksgiving. Notice the last one. A person who believes that something essential is still missing from their spiritual life will tend toward anxiety and striving. A person who knows that in Christ they have everything will overflow with gratitude. Few of Paul's letters return to thanksgiving as often for their length as Colossians does (1:3, 12; 2:7; 3:15, 16, 17; 4:2). Gratitude is the natural atmosphere of a life that is complete in Christ."]},{"heading":"Fullness, Forgiveness and the Defeat of the Powers","paragraphs":["Verse 8 is a warning with a vivid verb: “Be careful that you don’t let anyone rob you” — συλαγωγέω, to carry off as plunder, to kidnap. The false teaching is described as “philosophy and vain deceit, after the tradition of men, after the elements of the world, and not after Christ.” Paul is not condemning careful thinking; he is condemning a system of thought built on human tradition rather than Christ. The phrase “elements of the world,” στοιχεῖα τοῦ κόσμου, is honestly debated. It may mean the basic, elementary principles of religion (as in Galatians 4:3, 9, where Paul uses it of the law and pagan religion), or it may mean the elemental spiritual powers thought to control the universe. Given the angel worship of verse 18, the second meaning may well be in view; both lead to the same conclusion. Either way, it is “not after Christ.”","The reason follows: “For in him all the fullness of the Deity dwells bodily, and in him you are made full, who is the head of all principality and power” (vv. 9–10). The word θεότης means deity itself — the very nature of God, not merely divine qualities. All of it dwells in Christ “bodily,” in a real human body. And believers, united to Him, are “made full” — complete, lacking nothing. Then Paul lists what believers already have in Christ (vv. 11–13). They have been spiritually circumcised — the old sinful self cut away. They have been buried with Christ in baptism and raised with Him through faith in God's power. They were dead in trespasses and have been made alive with Christ. And they have been forgiven all their trespasses. There is nothing for the false teachers to add.","Verses 14 and 15 describe the cross in two astonishing images. First, the debt: God wiped out “the handwriting in ordinances which was against us,” the χειρόγραφον, a signed certificate of debt, and “has taken it out of the way, nailing it to the cross” (v. 14). Our record of disobedience was legally cancelled. Second, the triumph: “having stripped the principalities and the powers, he made a show of them openly, triumphing over them in it” (v. 15). The picture is a Roman triumph, with defeated enemies paraded through the streets. The two images belong together. The spiritual powers' greatest weapon against us was accusation — the record of our sin. When the debt was cancelled, they were disarmed. That is why believers need not fear them, worship them or seek them as mediators. They are defeated captives in Christ's procession."]},{"heading":"Shadows, Visions and Rules That Cannot Change the Heart","paragraphs":["Verses 16 to 23 apply this to the specific practices being pressed on the Colossians. First, religious observances: “Let no one therefore judge you in eating, or in drinking, or with respect to a feast day or a new moon or a Sabbath day, which are a shadow of the things to come; but the body is Christ’s” (vv. 16–17). These were real Old Testament institutions, given by God. Paul does not mock them. He places them in their proper role: shadows cast ahead by the coming Christ. Hebrews says the same — the law was “a shadow of the good to come, not the very image of the things” (Hebrews 10:1). Elsewhere Paul allows believers freedom to observe special days or not, as a matter of conscience (Romans 14:5–6). What he forbids is making them a test of spirituality or a ground to judge others.","Second, mystical experiences: “Let no one rob you of your prize by a voluntary humility and worshiping of the angels, dwelling in the things which he has not seen, vainly puffed up by his fleshly mind” (v. 18). The verb καταβραβεύω means to act as an umpire against someone, to disqualify them from a prize. Some in Colossae were apparently judging others as spiritually inferior because they lacked certain ascetic practices or visionary experiences involving angels. The phrase translated “dwelling in the things which he has not seen” is difficult; in the best manuscripts it reads “taking his stand on what he has seen” — visions — and the verb ἐμβατεύω was used in some mystery cults for entering a sanctuary after initiation. Paul's diagnosis is penetrating: such people are “puffed up,” and their real problem is that they are “not holding firmly to the Head” (v. 19). Spiritual growth does not come from visions or angels but from connection to Christ, “from whom all the body… grows with God’s growth.”","Third, ascetic rules: “Don’t handle, nor taste, nor touch” (v. 21). Paul's verdict is one of the most important statements in the New Testament about religious rule-keeping: “Which things indeed appear like wisdom in self-imposed worship, and humility, and severity to the body; but aren’t of any value against the indulgence of the flesh” (v. 23). Rules impress people. Harsh self-denial looks like serious spirituality. But external regulations cannot change the heart, and they cannot conquer the sinful nature. They often produce pride instead. Jesus said the same when He rebuked the Pharisees for “teaching as doctrines the commandments of men” and declared all foods clean, because what defiles a person comes from the heart, not the stomach (Mark 7:7, 18–19). The only power that changes the heart is union with Christ — which is exactly what chapter 3 will describe."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Testament Connection","paragraphs":["The burial and resurrection with Christ in baptism (v. 12) is developed in Romans: “Or don’t you know that all we who were baptized into Christ Jesus were baptized into his death? We were buried therefore with him through baptism into death, that just as Christ was raised from the dead through the glory of the Father, so we also might walk in newness of life” (Romans 6:3–4). And the movement from death in trespasses to being made alive with Christ (v. 13) is described in Ephesians, written in the same season: “You were made alive when you were dead in transgressions and sins” (Ephesians 2:1), and God, “even when we were dead through our trespasses, made us alive together with Christ (by grace you have been saved)” (Ephesians 2:5). The same verb, συζωοποιέω, appears in both letters and nowhere else in the New Testament.","Christ's defeat of the powers at the cross (v. 15) is a theme across the New Testament. Jesus said, as His death approached, “Now is the judgment of this world. Now the prince of this world will be cast out” (John 12:31). Hebrews explains that He shared our humanity “that through death he might bring to nothing him who had the power of death, that is, the devil” (Hebrews 2:14). And John writes, “To this end the Son of God was revealed: that he might destroy the works of the devil” (1 John 3:8). The worship of angels condemned in verse 18 is also rejected in Revelation, where John twice falls down before an angel, and is told: “Look! Don’t do it! I am a fellow bondservant with you and with your brothers who hold the testimony of Jesus. Worship God” (Revelation 19:10).","The teaching on shadows and human regulations (vv. 16–23) echoes Jesus' own words. Quoting Isaiah, He said, “they worship me in vain, teaching as doctrines the commandments of men” (Mark 7:7), and taught that “whatever goes into the man from outside can’t defile him, because it doesn’t go into his heart, but into his stomach” (Mark 7:18–19). Paul warns Timothy against those who forbid marriage and are “commanding to abstain from foods which God created to be received with thanksgiving… For every creature of God is good, and nothing is to be rejected, if it is received with thanksgiving” (1 Timothy 4:3–4). And Hebrews calls the earthly tabernacle “a copy and shadow of the heavenly things” (Hebrews 8:5). The New Testament speaks with one voice: Christ is the substance; rules and rituals cannot add to Him."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Rob / carry off as plunder","hebrew":"συλαγωγέω","translit":"sylagōgéō","strongs":"G4812","verses":"v. 8","insight":"Strong's: “to lead away as booty, i.e. (figuratively) seduce.” This is the only occurrence of the word in the New Testament. It combines σύλη, plunder, and ἄγω, to lead: to drag off captives as spoils of war. Paul pictures false teachers as raiders who kidnap believers and carry them away from Christ. The weapons are not swords but “philosophy and vain deceit, after the tradition of men.” The danger is real: believers can be carried off by persuasive ideas that sound spiritual but lead away from Christ. The defence is to be “rooted and built up in him” (v. 7) — firmly attached, hard to carry off."},{"english":"Deity / Godhead","hebrew":"θεότης","translit":"theótēs","strongs":"G2320 (with σωματικῶς, sōmatikōs, G4985)","verses":"v. 9","insight":"Strong's defines θεότης as “divinity (abstractly),” and σωματικῶς as “corporeally or physically.” This is the only occurrence of θεότης in the New Testament. A related word, θειότης (Romans 1:20), refers to divine qualities seen in creation. Θεότης is stronger: the very essence and being of God. Paul says all the fullness of this deity dwells in Christ, and dwells bodily. This is one of the clearest affirmations in the New Testament that Jesus is fully God, and that He is fully God in a human body. The God who cannot be seen became touchable. And verse 10 adds: in Him, you are made full."},{"english":"Handwritten document / certificate of debt","hebrew":"χειρόγραφον","translit":"cheirógraphon","strongs":"G5498","verses":"v. 14","insight":"Strong's: “something hand-written (‘chirograph’), i.e. a manuscript (specially, a legal document or bond (figuratively)).” This is its only occurrence in the New Testament. In the papyri, χειρόγραφον commonly meant a certificate of debt, written or signed by the debtor, acknowledging what he owed. Paul says the record of our debt to God, “with its ordinances,” stood against us — and Christ “wiped out” the writing and “nailed it to the cross.” Some readers see an allusion to the charge notice nailed above Jesus' head on the cross. The point is the same: what condemned us was nailed up with Him and cancelled forever."},{"english":"Triumph / lead in a triumphal procession","hebrew":"θριαμβεύω","translit":"thriambeúō","strongs":"G2358","verses":"v. 15 (and 2 Corinthians 2:14)","insight":"Strong's notes it is related to a hymn sung in festal processions. In Roman usage θριαμβεύω meant to celebrate a triumph: the victorious general rode through the city with his army, his spoils and his captured enemies paraded behind him in chains. Paul uses it only twice: in 2 Corinthians 2:14, where God leads believers in Christ's triumphal procession, and here, where Christ leads the defeated spiritual powers. The cross looked like Christ's defeat; it was the moment of His victory parade. The powers that once held humanity captive are now themselves captives, exposed and disarmed."},{"english":"Shadow","hebrew":"σκιά","translit":"skiá","strongs":"G4639","verses":"v. 17 (and Hebrews 8:5; 10:1)","insight":"Strong's: “‘shade’ or a shadow (literally or figuratively (darkness of error or an adumbration)).” A shadow has the shape of the real thing but not its substance. Paul says the Old Testament food laws, festivals, new moons and Sabbaths were “a shadow of the things to come; but the body is Christ’s.” Hebrews uses the same contrast: the earthly tabernacle was “a copy and shadow of the heavenly things” (8:5), and the law had “a shadow of the good to come” (10:1). Shadows are not bad; they point to the reality. But it would be foolish to cling to the shadow when the body casting it has arrived. Every shadow in the Old Testament falls from the shape of Christ."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the additions you have made to Christ, the debt you are still trying to pay, and the rules you are trusting to change your heart.","items":[{"title":"Christ Plus What?","body":"Every generation has its version of the Colossian heresy: Christ plus a special experience, Christ plus a set of rules, Christ plus a spiritual technique, Christ plus a hidden teaching, Christ plus a particular diet, discipline or calendar. And every one of them carries the same message: you are not complete yet. The Spirit wants to say to you clearly: “in him you are made full.” Is there something you have quietly added to Christ as necessary for being a real or mature believer? It may be a good thing in itself. But as an addition, it is a shadow. Lay it down, and walk in Christ the way you received Him."},{"title":"The Debt Is Already Nailed Up","body":"Some believers live as though they are still working off a debt to God. Every failure feels like another entry on the account, and every spiritual activity feels like a payment. The Spirit is showing you the cross with a document nailed to it: your certificate of debt, with every charge written in your own handwriting — wiped clean and nailed up. It was cancelled. Permanently. You are not paying it off. You are living in the freedom of someone whose debt has been forgiven. Today, every time you feel the weight of guilt over something already confessed, say: “It was nailed to the cross. It is cancelled.”"},{"title":"Rules That Cannot Reach Your Heart","body":"You may have tried to deal with a persistent sin by making stricter rules for yourself — more restrictions, more effort, more self-punishment. And it has not worked, or it has worked only for a while. Paul tells you why: such rules “aren’t of any value against the indulgence of the flesh.” They can restrain behaviour from the outside, but they cannot change the heart from the inside. The Spirit is not telling you to give up self-control. He is telling you that your power against sin is not in the rules but in your union with Christ: you died with Him, you were raised with Him. Hold fast to the Head. Chapter 3 will show you what that looks like."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Addition Check","body":"Write down anything you have felt you need, beyond faith in Christ, to be accepted by God or to be a “real” believer — a practice, an experience, a rule, a teaching, a group. Then read verses 9 and 10 aloud. Cross out anything you have been treating as an addition to Christ."},{"title":"The Debt Check","body":"Are there sins you have confessed and still feel you are paying for? Write one down. Then write over it, in large letters, “NAILED TO THE CROSS” (v. 14). Ask yourself why you keep picking up a document God has cancelled."},{"title":"The Judging Check","body":"Have you ever looked down on another believer because their practices — worship style, holy days, diet, devotional habits, spiritual experiences — were different from yours? Or have you let someone make you feel inferior because of theirs? Name it. Verse 16 says: let no one judge you, and by implication, judge no one this way."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Rooted and Overflowing","body":"Verse 7 describes a believer “rooted and built up in him… abounding in it in thanksgiving.” For the next seven days, end each day by writing down five specific things you have in Christ that you did nothing to earn — drawn from verses 9 to 15: complete in Him; circumcised in heart; buried and raised with Him; made alive; forgiven all trespasses; debt cancelled; the powers defeated. Then thank God aloud for each one. Notice whether gratitude crowds out the sense that you still need something more."},{"title":"Holding the Head","body":"Verse 19 says the problem with false spirituality is “not holding firmly to the Head.” This week, before you do any religious activity — prayer, Bible reading, church, giving, fasting — pause and say: “Lord Jesus, I am not doing this to become complete; I am complete in You. I am doing this to know You.” Watch how the activity changes from a performance into a relationship. If any practice in your life cannot be done in that spirit, ask whether it has become an addition."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge are hidden in You. Keep me from being carried off by philosophy and empty deceit, by the traditions of men and the elemental things of the world. As I received You, so let me walk in You — rooted, built up, established in the faith, overflowing with thanksgiving. In You all the fullness of God dwells bodily, and in You I am made full. I need nothing added to You. Thank You that in You I have been circumcised with a circumcision not made with hands, that I was buried with You in baptism and raised with You through faith in the working of God. I was dead in my trespasses, and You made me alive with You and forgave me all my trespasses. You wiped out the record of debt that stood against me, with all its demands, and You took it away, nailing it to the cross. You disarmed the rulers and authorities and made a public spectacle of them, triumphing over them in the cross. I will not fear them, and I will not worship them. Let no one judge me by shadows, for You are the substance. Let no one disqualify me with visions and false humility. Keep me holding fast to You, my Head. And free me from trusting in rules that cannot change my heart. You alone can. In Your name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Unwashed Hands and the Tradition of the Elders","ref":"Mark 7:1-23","relatedVerses":"v. 8, 16, 20-23","summary":"Pharisees and scribes from Jerusalem noticed that some of Jesus' disciples ate with unwashed hands — not unhygienic, but without the ceremonial washing their tradition required. They asked why His disciples did not walk according to the tradition of the elders. Jesus answered by quoting Isaiah: this people honours God with their lips but their heart is far from Him, teaching as doctrines the commandments of men. Then He explained to the crowd that nothing going into a person from outside can defile them, because it goes into the stomach and not the heart; what defiles comes out of the heart. Mark adds that in saying this, Jesus declared all foods clean. Colossians 2 is the same battle in a Gentile city: human traditions about food and observance offered as spirituality, and the answer that only a changed heart matters."},{"title":"John Falls at the Angel's Feet","ref":"Revelation 19:9-10; 22:8-9","relatedVerses":"v. 18-19","summary":"At the end of the visions given to John on Patmos, the apostle was so overwhelmed by the angel who showed him these things that he fell at his feet to worship him. The angel stopped him at once: do not do it; I am a fellow servant with you and your brothers; worship God. It happened twice. If even the beloved apostle could be drawn toward reverencing an angel in a moment of awe, the danger in Colossae was real. The angel's response is the right one: angels are fellow servants, created beings, not mediators to be worshipped. Christ is the head over every ruler and authority (Colossians 2:10), and worship belongs to God alone."},{"title":"Hezekiah Breaks the Bronze Serpent","ref":"Numbers 21:4-9; 2 Kings 18:1-7","relatedVerses":"v. 16-17","summary":"In the wilderness, God told Moses to make a bronze serpent so that everyone bitten by the fiery serpents could look at it and live. It was a God-given sign, and Jesus later said it pointed to His own death (John 3:14). But centuries later the people of Judah were burning incense to it, and they had given it a name, Nehushtan. King Hezekiah, who trusted in the LORD, broke it in pieces. A good thing God had given as a pointer to salvation had become an object of devotion in itself. That is the danger Colossians 2 addresses: God-given shadows — festivals, food laws, Sabbaths — turned into the substance. Hezekiah did not despise what Moses made; he refused to let it take the place of God. Christ is the reality every shadow pointed to."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Colossians","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Raised With Christ — Put Off the Old, Put On the New, and Do Everything in His Name","themeTitle":"“Your Life Is Hidden With Christ — So Live From Where You Really Are”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"If then you were raised together with Christ, seek the things that are above, where Christ is, seated on the right hand of God.","understanding":"The word εἰ, ‘if’, here means ‘since’: Paul is not doubting that they were raised, he is building on it. συνηγέρθητε, you were raised together, picks up 2:12, where they were raised with Christ through faith. Because that is true, ζητεῖτε, keep seeking, the things ἄνω, above. The present tense means a continual pursuit. And the place is defined by a Person: “where Christ is, seated on the right hand of God.” The right hand is the seat of authority from Psalm 110:1, the most quoted Old Testament verse in the New Testament. The Christian life starts with where Jesus is sitting.","words":[{"strongs":"G4891","hebrew":"συνεγείρω","translit":"synegeírō","english":"raised together"},{"strongs":"G2212","hebrew":"ζητέω","translit":"zētéō","english":"seek"},{"strongs":"G507","hebrew":"ἄνω","translit":"ánō","english":"above"},{"strongs":"G1188","hebrew":"δεξιός","translit":"dexiós","english":"right hand"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"seated"}]},{"v":2,"text":"Set your mind on the things that are above, not on the things that are on the earth.","understanding":"Verse 1 says seek; verse 2 says φρονεῖτε, set your mind — direct your thinking, your attitude, your inner orientation. The same verb appears in Philippians 2:5, ‘have this mind in you’. Seeking is the pursuit; setting the mind is the steady thought-life behind it. Paul contrasts the things above with τὰ ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς, the things on the earth. He does not mean that creation is evil. He means the whole realm of the rules and regulations of chapter 2 and the sins of verses 5–9: everything that belongs to life apart from Christ.","words":[{"strongs":"G5426","hebrew":"φρονέω","translit":"phronéō","english":"set your mind"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"}]},{"v":3,"text":"For you died, and your life is hidden with Christ in God.","understanding":"Here is the reason: “For you died.” The aorist ἀπεθάνετε points to a completed event — their death with Christ (2:20). And your ζωή, life, κέκρυπται, has been hidden, perfect tense: it was hidden and it remains hidden. Your real life is kept safe with Christ in God, hidden from the world's sight and beyond the reach of any enemy. The false teachers spoke of hidden mysteries; Paul says the most precious hidden thing is your own life, stored with Christ.","words":[{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"died"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G2928","hebrew":"κρύπτω","translit":"krýptō","english":"hidden"}]},{"v":4,"text":"When Christ, our life, is revealed, then you will also be revealed with him in glory.","understanding":"What is hidden will not stay hidden. When Christ is φανερωθῇ, revealed, made visible — at His return — you will also be revealed with Him ἐν δόξῃ, in glory. Notice the title: “Christ, our life.” Christ is not only the giver of life; He is the life. Our future is tied to His appearing. Today the world does not see who believers really are; on that day it will. As John says, “when he is revealed, we will be like him” (1 John 3:2).","words":[{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Put to death therefore your members which are on the earth: sexual immorality, uncleanness, depraved passion, evil desire, and covetousness, which is idolatry;","understanding":"Now the practical consequence: νεκρώσατε, put to death. The aorist imperative calls for decisive action. You died with Christ (v. 3); now treat sin as something already dead. Your μέλη, members, “which are on the earth” means your earthly ways of using the body. Then comes a list moving from outward acts to inner desires: πορνεία, sexual immorality; ἀκαθαρσία, uncleanness; πάθος, depraved passion; ἐπιθυμία κακή, evil desire; and πλεονεξία, covetousness — literally ‘wanting more’. Paul calls covetousness εἰδωλολατρία, idolatry, because whatever you crave above God becomes your god.","words":[{"strongs":"G3499","hebrew":"νεκρόω","translit":"nekróō","english":"put to death"},{"strongs":"G3196","hebrew":"μέλος","translit":"mélos","english":"members"},{"strongs":"G4202","hebrew":"πορνεία","translit":"porneía","english":"sexual immorality"},{"strongs":"G167","hebrew":"ἀκαθαρσία","translit":"akatharsía","english":"uncleanness"},{"strongs":"G3806","hebrew":"πάθος","translit":"páthos","english":"passion"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"desire"},{"strongs":"G2556","hebrew":"κακός","translit":"kakós","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G4124","hebrew":"πλεονεξία","translit":"pleonexía","english":"covetousness"},{"strongs":"G1495","hebrew":"εἰδωλολατρεία","translit":"eidōlolatreía","english":"idolatry"}]},{"v":6,"text":"for which things’ sake the wrath of God comes on the children of disobedience.","understanding":"These are not small matters. Because of them “the wrath of God comes on the children of disobedience.” ὀργή is God's settled, holy opposition to evil, not a sudden temper. The present tense ‘comes’ means it is coming and certain. The phrase ‘sons of disobedience’ (υἱοὺς τῆς ἀπειθείας) is a Hebrew idiom: people whose character is defined by disobedience. Believers are not under that wrath, but they must not make peace with the things that bring it.","words":[{"strongs":"G3709","hebrew":"ὀργή","translit":"orgḗ","english":"wrath"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G543","hebrew":"ἀπείθεια","translit":"apeítheia","english":"disobedience"}]},{"v":7,"text":"You also once walked in those, when you lived in them;","understanding":"Paul is honest about their past: “You also once walked in those, when you lived in them.” περιεπατήσατε, you walked, is Paul's word for a pattern of life. ἐζῆτε, you lived, describes the atmosphere they breathed. This is not shame-mongering; it is a reminder of how far grace has brought them. ‘Once’ marks a boundary. That was then.","words":[{"strongs":"G4043","hebrew":"περιπατέω","translit":"peripatéō","english":"walked"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"lived"}]},{"v":8,"text":"but now you also put them all away: anger, wrath, malice, slander, and shameful speaking out of your mouth.","understanding":"“But now” — the turning point of the gospel. ἀπόθεσθε, put away, is the word for taking off clothing. Lay these aside like filthy garments. The second list moves to sins of temper and tongue: ὀργή, anger (settled anger); θυμός, wrath (a sudden outburst); κακία, malice, ill will that wants harm for others; βλασφημία, slander, speech that tears people down; and αἰσχρολογία, shameful speaking, foul and abusive talk “out of your mouth.” The list ends at the mouth, because the heart overflows there.","words":[{"strongs":"G659","hebrew":"ἀποτίθημι","translit":"apotíthēmi","english":"put away"},{"strongs":"G3709","hebrew":"ὀργή","translit":"orgḗ","english":"anger"},{"strongs":"G2372","hebrew":"θυμός","translit":"thymós","english":"wrath"},{"strongs":"G2549","hebrew":"κακία","translit":"kakía","english":"malice"},{"strongs":"G988","hebrew":"βλασφημία","translit":"blasphēmía","english":"slander"},{"strongs":"G148","hebrew":"αἰσχρολογία","translit":"aischrología","english":"shameful speaking"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"}]},{"v":9,"text":"Don’t lie to one another, seeing that you have put off the old man with his doings,","understanding":"μὴ ψεύδεσθε, “Don’t lie to one another.” Lying destroys the fellowship of a body (compare Ephesians 4:25: “we are members of one another”). The reason given is clothing language: ἀπεκδυσάμενοι, having stripped off, the παλαιὸν ἄνθρωπον, the old man, “with his doings” (πράξεσιν, practices). The ‘old man’ is who you were in Adam, before Christ. That person was crucified with Christ (Romans 6:6). Lying belongs to his wardrobe, not yours.","words":[{"strongs":"G5574","hebrew":"ψεύδομαι","translit":"pseúdomai","english":"lie"},{"strongs":"G554","hebrew":"ἀπεκδύομαι","translit":"apekdýomai","english":"put off"},{"strongs":"G3820","hebrew":"παλαιός","translit":"palaiós","english":"old"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G4234","hebrew":"πρᾶξις","translit":"prâxis","english":"doings"}]},{"v":10,"text":"and have put on the new man, who is being renewed in knowledge after the image of his Creator,","understanding":"And you have ἐνδυσάμενοι, put on, τὸν νέον, the new man. The new self is not finished: it is ἀνακαινούμενον, being renewed, present tense, continually made new, εἰς ἐπίγνωσιν, into full knowledge. The pattern is “the image of his Creator” — an echo of Genesis 1, where humanity was made in God's image. Sin defaced that image; in Christ, who is “the image of the invisible God” (1:15), it is being restored. Sanctification is God re-creating you to look like Jesus.","words":[{"strongs":"G1746","hebrew":"ἐνδύω","translit":"endýō","english":"put on"},{"strongs":"G3501","hebrew":"νέος","translit":"néos","english":"new"},{"strongs":"G341","hebrew":"ἀνακαινόω","translit":"anakainóō","english":"being renewed"},{"strongs":"G1922","hebrew":"ἐπίγνωσις","translit":"epígnōsis","english":"knowledge"},{"strongs":"G1504","hebrew":"εἰκών","translit":"eikṓn","english":"image"},{"strongs":"G2936","hebrew":"κτίζω","translit":"ktízō","english":"Creator"}]},{"v":11,"text":"where there can’t be Greek and Jew, circumcision and uncircumcision, barbarian, Scythian, bondservant, freeman; but Christ is all, and in all.","understanding":"In this new humanity the old walls fall. Ἕλλην and Ἰουδαῖος, Greek and Jew, the great religious divide; περιτομή and ἀκροβυστία, circumcision and uncircumcision, the ritual divide the false teachers stressed (2:11); βάρβαρος, barbarian, anyone who did not speak Greek; Σκύθης, Scythian, whom the ancient world regarded as the most savage of all; δοῦλος and ἐλεύθερος, bondservant and freeman, the social divide. None of these define the believer, because “Christ is all, and in all.” He is everything that matters, and He lives in every one of them.","words":[{"strongs":"G1672","hebrew":"Ἕλλην","translit":"Héllēn","english":"Greek"},{"strongs":"G2453","hebrew":"Ἰουδαῖος","translit":"Ioudaîos","english":"Jew"},{"strongs":"G4061","hebrew":"περιτομή","translit":"peritomḗ","english":"circumcision"},{"strongs":"G203","hebrew":"ἀκροβυστία","translit":"akrobystía","english":"uncircumcision"},{"strongs":"G915","hebrew":"βάρβαρος","translit":"bárbaros","english":"barbarian"},{"strongs":"G4658","hebrew":"Σκύθης","translit":"Skýthēs","english":"Scythian"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"bondservant"},{"strongs":"G1658","hebrew":"ἐλεύθερος","translit":"eleútheros","english":"freeman"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Put on therefore, as God’s chosen ones, holy and beloved, a heart of compassion, kindness, lowliness, humility, and perseverance;","understanding":"Now the new wardrobe. Put on “therefore” — because of who you are. Three titles, all once used of Israel, now given to believers: ἐκλεκτοί, chosen; ἅγιοι, holy; ἠγαπημένοι, beloved (perfect tense: loved and still loved). Identity comes first; behaviour follows. Then five garments: σπλάγχνα οἰκτιρμοῦ, a heart of compassion (literally ‘bowels of mercy’, deep gut-level pity); χρηστότης, kindness; ταπεινοφροσύνη, lowliness; πραΰτης, humility or gentleness; and μακροθυμία, perseverance, being long-tempered rather than short-tempered. Every one of them is a quality of Jesus Himself.","words":[{"strongs":"G1588","hebrew":"ἐκλεκτός","translit":"eklektós","english":"chosen ones"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G4698","hebrew":"σπλάγχνον","translit":"splánchnon","english":"heart"},{"strongs":"G3628","hebrew":"οἰκτιρμός","translit":"oiktirmós","english":"compassion"},{"strongs":"G5544","hebrew":"χρηστότης","translit":"chrēstótēs","english":"kindness"},{"strongs":"G5012","hebrew":"ταπεινοφροσύνη","translit":"tapeinophrosýnē","english":"lowliness"},{"strongs":"G4236","hebrew":"πρᾳότης","translit":"praiótēs","english":"humility"},{"strongs":"G3115","hebrew":"μακροθυμία","translit":"makrothymía","english":"perseverance"}]},{"v":13,"text":"bearing with one another, and forgiving each other, if any man has a complaint against any; even as Christ forgave you, so you also do.","understanding":"ἀνεχόμενοι, “bearing with one another” — putting up with people's weaknesses and irritating habits. χαριζόμενοι, forgiving — a word built on χάρις, grace: forgiveness is grace given freely. “If any man has a complaint” (μομφή, a cause for blame) — Paul assumes Christians will have real grievances. The standard is not how sorry the person is or how small the offence is, but how Christ treated you: “even as Christ forgave you, so you also do.” The forgiven forgive.","words":[{"strongs":"G430","hebrew":"ἀνέχομαι","translit":"anéchomai","english":"bearing with"},{"strongs":"G5483","hebrew":"χαρίζομαι","translit":"charízomai","english":"forgiving"},{"strongs":"G3437","hebrew":"μομφή","translit":"momphḗ","english":"complaint"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Above all these things, walk in love, which is the bond of perfection.","understanding":"“Above all these things” — over all the other garments, like an outer belt or coat — put on ἀγάπη, love. Love is σύνδεσμος, the bond, the ligament that ties everything together (the same word used of the body's ligaments in 2:19). Without love the virtues fall apart; with love they become τελειότης, perfection, completeness, maturity. The false teachers offered perfection through knowledge; Paul says it comes through love.","words":[{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G4886","hebrew":"σύνδεσμος","translit":"sýndesmos","english":"bond"},{"strongs":"G5047","hebrew":"τελειότης","translit":"teleiótēs","english":"perfection"}]},{"v":15,"text":"And let the peace of God rule in your hearts, to which also you were called in one body; and be thankful.","understanding":"“Let the peace of God rule in your hearts” — the best manuscripts read ‘peace of Christ’. βραβευέτω means to act as umpire, the referee at the games who decides the outcome. When you face a decision or a conflict, let Christ's peace make the call. This is peace “to which also you were called in one body,” so it is a peace between believers, not only within one. And then a short command: εὐχάριστοι γίνεσθε, “be thankful.” Gratitude is the soil in which peace grows.","words":[{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"},{"strongs":"G1018","hebrew":"βραβεύω","translit":"brabeúō","english":"rule"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"hearts"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G2170","hebrew":"εὐχάριστος","translit":"eucháristos","english":"thankful"}]},{"v":16,"text":"Let the word of Christ dwell in you richly; in all wisdom teaching and admonishing one another with psalms, hymns, and spiritual songs, singing with grace in your heart to the Lord.","understanding":"“Let the word of Christ dwell in you richly.” ἐνοικείτω means to take up residence, to live at home. Christ's word is not to be a guest you visit on Sundays; it should live in your house, πλουσίως, richly, abundantly. When it does, the church becomes a teaching community: διδάσκοντες, teaching, and νουθετοῦντες, admonishing, warning one another. And it becomes a singing community: ψαλμοί, psalms; ὕμνοι, hymns of praise; ᾠδαὶ πνευματικαί, spiritual songs. ᾄδοντες, singing, “with grace in your heart to the Lord.” The word of Christ fills the church with both instruction and song.","words":[{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G1774","hebrew":"ἐνοικέω","translit":"enoikéō","english":"dwell"},{"strongs":"G4146","hebrew":"πλουσίως","translit":"plousíōs","english":"richly"},{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G1321","hebrew":"διδάσκω","translit":"didáskō","english":"teaching"},{"strongs":"G3560","hebrew":"νουθετέω","translit":"nouthetéō","english":"admonishing"},{"strongs":"G5568","hebrew":"ψαλμός","translit":"psalmós","english":"psalms"},{"strongs":"G5215","hebrew":"ὕμνος","translit":"hýmnos","english":"hymns"},{"strongs":"G5603","hebrew":"ᾠδή","translit":"ōidḗ","english":"songs"},{"strongs":"G4152","hebrew":"πνευματικός","translit":"pneumatikós","english":"spiritual"},{"strongs":"G103","hebrew":"ᾄδω","translit":"áidō","english":"singing"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"}]},{"v":17,"text":"Whatever you do, in word or in deed, do all in the name of the Lord Jesus, giving thanks to God the Father, through him.","understanding":"The summary of the whole section: “Whatever you do, in word or in deed” — λόγῳ ἢ ἔργῳ, everything said and everything done. Do it all ἐν ὀνόματι, in the name of the Lord Jesus — as His representative, by His authority, for His reputation. Paul does not divide life into sacred and secular. Washing dishes, writing a report, speaking to a neighbour can all be done in His name. And again, gratitude: εὐχαριστοῦντες, giving thanks to God the Father through Him. Thanksgiving is mentioned three times in verses 15–17.","words":[{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"deed"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G2168","hebrew":"εὐχαριστέω","translit":"eucharistéō","english":"giving thanks"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Wives, be in subjection to your husbands, as is fitting in the Lord.","understanding":"Now Paul applies the new life to the household, in a pattern called a ‘household code’. Unlike the codes of Greek philosophers, which spoke only to the man in charge, Paul addresses every member directly, as a moral person. γυναῖκες, wives; ὑποτάσσεσθε, be in subjection — a voluntary arranging of oneself under, the same word used of Christ submitting to the Father (1 Corinthians 15:28). The qualifier transforms it: “as is fitting in the Lord.” ἀνῆκεν, fitting, proper. This is submission inside a relationship with Christ, never submission to sin.","words":[{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"wives"},{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"be in subjection"},{"strongs":"G435","hebrew":"ἀνήρ","translit":"anḗr","english":"husbands"},{"strongs":"G433","hebrew":"ἀνήκω","translit":"anḗkō","english":"fitting"}]},{"v":19,"text":"Husbands, love your wives, and don’t be bitter against them.","understanding":"ἄνδρες, husbands: ἀγαπᾶτε, love your wives. In a culture where a husband had legal power over his wife, Paul commands not authority but ἀγάπη, self-giving love. And he adds a specific prohibition: μὴ πικραίνεσθε, “don’t be bitter against them.” πικραίνω means to make bitter or to be embittered — harsh, resentful, sour. A husband's love is tested in the small irritations of daily life.","words":[{"strongs":"G435","hebrew":"ἀνήρ","translit":"anḗr","english":"husbands"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"wives"},{"strongs":"G4087","hebrew":"πικραίνω","translit":"pikraínō","english":"be bitter"}]},{"v":20,"text":"Children, obey your parents in all things, for this pleases the Lord.","understanding":"τέκνα, children, are addressed as members of the church who would hear this letter read aloud. ὑπακούετε, obey — literally ‘hear under’, listen and respond. The reason: “for this pleases the Lord” (εὐάρεστον, well-pleasing). Children's obedience is not only good manners; it is worship. Even the youngest believer can please the Lord.","words":[{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G5219","hebrew":"ὑπακούω","translit":"hypakoúō","english":"obey"},{"strongs":"G1118","hebrew":"γονεύς","translit":"goneús","english":"parents"},{"strongs":"G2101","hebrew":"εὐάρεστος","translit":"euárestos","english":"pleases"}]},{"v":21,"text":"Fathers, don’t provoke your children, so that they won’t be discouraged.","understanding":"πατέρες, fathers, μὴ ἐρεθίζετε, don't provoke, stir up, exasperate your children. Constant criticism, unfair demands, harshness and favouritism all provoke. The result is that they ἀθυμῶσιν, lose heart, become spiritless and discouraged. Roman fathers had near-absolute power over their children; Paul limits that power by the child's heart. Parental authority exists to build children up, not to crush them.","words":[{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"fathers"},{"strongs":"G2042","hebrew":"ἐρεθίζω","translit":"erethízō","english":"provoke"},{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G120","hebrew":"ἀθυμέω","translit":"athyméō","english":"be discouraged"}]},{"v":22,"text":"Servants, obey in all things those who are your masters according to the flesh, not just when they are looking, as men pleasers, but in singleness of heart, fearing God.","understanding":"The longest instruction goes to δοῦλοι, servants or slaves, who made up a large part of the early church. Onesimus, a runaway slave from Colossae, was travelling with this letter (4:9). Obey your earthly κύριοι, masters, “not just when they are looking” — ὀφθαλμοδουλία, ‘eye-service’, working only when watched; “as men pleasers” — ἀνθρωπάρεσκοι, people who live for human approval. Instead, ἐν ἁπλότητι καρδίας, “in singleness of heart,” undivided sincerity, φοβούμενοι τὸν κύριον, fearing the Lord. The real Master is always watching.","words":[{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"servants"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"masters"},{"strongs":"G3787","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμοδουλεία","translit":"ophthalmodouleía","english":"eye-service"},{"strongs":"G441","hebrew":"ἀνθρωπάρεσκος","translit":"anthrōpáreskos","english":"men pleasers"},{"strongs":"G572","hebrew":"ἁπλότης","translit":"haplótēs","english":"singleness"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"heart"},{"strongs":"G5399","hebrew":"φοβέω","translit":"phobéō","english":"fearing"}]},{"v":23,"text":"And whatever you do, work heartily, as for the Lord, and not for men,","understanding":"“And whatever you do, work heartily” — ἐκ ψυχῆς ἐργάζεσθε, literally ‘work from the soul’. Put your whole self into it. “As for the Lord, and not for men.” This one sentence turned the ancient world's view of labour upside down. The lowest task became an offering to Christ. No work is too humble when it is done for Him.","words":[{"strongs":"G2038","hebrew":"ἐργάζομαι","translit":"ergázomai","english":"work"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"heartily"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"}]},{"v":24,"text":"knowing that from the Lord you will receive the reward of the inheritance; for you serve the Lord Christ.","understanding":"The motive is future: ἀπολήμψεσθε, you will receive in full, from the Lord τὴν ἀνταπόδοσιν, the repayment, “of the inheritance.” This is remarkable, because under Roman law a slave could not inherit anything. Yet Paul tells these slaves that they are heirs. Then the key line: τῷ κυρίῳ Χριστῷ δουλεύετε, “you serve the Lord Christ.” Their true employer is Jesus.","words":[{"strongs":"G618","hebrew":"ἀπολαμβάνω","translit":"apolambánō","english":"receive"},{"strongs":"G469","hebrew":"ἀνταπόδοσις","translit":"antapódosis","english":"reward"},{"strongs":"G2817","hebrew":"κληρονομία","translit":"klēronomía","english":"inheritance"},{"strongs":"G1398","hebrew":"δουλεύω","translit":"douleúō","english":"serve"}]},{"v":25,"text":"But he who does wrong will receive again for the wrong that he has done, and there is no partiality.","understanding":"“But he who does wrong (ἀδικῶν) will receive again (κομίσεται) for the wrong that he has done.” Justice cuts both ways. A slave who cheats will answer for it — and so will a master who abuses (see 4:1). “And there is no partiality” — προσωπολημψία, literally ‘receiving the face’, judging by appearance or status. Before God, the master's rank gives no protection and the slave's low position is no excuse. Everyone stands before the same just Judge.","words":[{"strongs":"G91","hebrew":"ἀδικέω","translit":"adikéō","english":"does wrong"},{"strongs":"G2865","hebrew":"κομίζω","translit":"komízō","english":"receive again"},{"strongs":"G4382","hebrew":"προσωποληψία","translit":"prosōpolēpsía","english":"partiality"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Life in the Vault","verseRef":"v. 3-4","image":"Picture a deep vault, sealed and guarded, in the throne room of heaven. Inside it lies a single treasure, kept safe: your life. Not the life the world sees — your job, your reputation, your struggles, your age — but your true life, the person God made you to be in Christ. The door of the vault has two names written on it: Christ, and God. Nothing on earth can break in. No accuser, no illness, no failure, no death can reach what is inside. Now picture the day the vault opens. Christ steps out into the sight of all creation, and as He is revealed, you are revealed with Him — the hidden life finally visible, shining with His glory.","meaning":"Paul says “your life is hidden with Christ in God” (v. 3). Hidden means two things: kept safe, and not yet seen. Your real identity is not on display yet. People may misunderstand you, circumstances may hide it, and even you may struggle to see it. But it is secure, because it is held inside the relationship between the Father and the Son. And it will not stay hidden for ever: “When Christ, our life, is revealed, then you will also be revealed with him in glory” (v. 4). The believer lives now from a secure, hidden place, and looks forward to a glorious unveiling."},{"title":"The Changing Room","verseRef":"v. 8-14","image":"Picture a person entering a changing room wearing filthy, torn clothes that still smell of the grave: anger, malice, slander, lies, foul speech. One by one the garments come off and are thrown out — they no longer fit, they belong to someone who died. Hanging on the wall is a new set of clothes, cut to the measure of Christ Himself: compassion, kindness, lowliness, gentleness, patience. The person puts them on one at a time. Then, over everything, comes a final garment — a broad belt of love that ties the whole outfit together so that nothing slips. The person steps out looking strangely like Jesus.","meaning":"Paul uses clothing language throughout this chapter: “put them all away” (v. 8), “you have put off the old man” (v. 9), “have put on the new man” (v. 10), “Put on therefore” (v. 12), and “Above all these things, walk in love, which is the bond of perfection” (v. 14). Changing your character is not mainly about effort to improve the old clothes; it is about taking off what belongs to the dead self and putting on what belongs to the new self. This happened decisively when you came to Christ, and you live it out daily, choice by choice. And love is the belt: without it, every other virtue comes loose."},{"title":"The Umpire's Whistle","verseRef":"v. 15","image":"Picture a busy stadium in an ancient city. Two athletes are disputing a result; the crowd is shouting, the competitors are angry, and everyone has an opinion. Then the umpire steps forward, raises his hand and gives his decision, and the matter is settled. Now picture your own heart as that stadium: anxious thoughts, competing voices, hurt feelings, a decision you do not know how to make, a quarrel with a fellow believer. In the middle stands the peace of Christ, holding the authority to make the call. When His peace is present, you go forward; when it is disturbed, you stop and listen.","meaning":"The verb in verse 15 is βραβεύω, to act as umpire. Paul says, “let the peace of God rule in your hearts.” Christ's peace is meant to be the deciding voice in the life of the believer and of the church. It does not replace Scripture or wisdom, but it guards both. When an action or attitude disturbs the peace Christ gives — and especially the peace of the body “to which also you were called in one body” — that is a signal to stop. Paul immediately adds “and be thankful,” because a grateful heart is the easiest place for Christ's peace to rule."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Seek the Things Above (v. 1-4)","body":"Because believers were raised with Christ, they must seek and set their minds on the things above, where Christ is enthroned; they died, their life is hidden with Christ in God, and when Christ appears they will appear with Him in glory."},{"label":"Put to Death and Put Away (v. 5-11)","body":"Put to death earthly sins of desire and covetousness, which bring God's wrath; put away sins of temper and tongue, and stop lying, because the old man has been put off and the new man put on — a new humanity being renewed in the image of its Creator, where every human division gives way to Christ who is all and in all."},{"label":"Put On the New Wardrobe (v. 12-14)","body":"As God's chosen, holy and beloved people, put on compassion, kindness, lowliness, humility and patience; bear with one another and forgive as Christ forgave; and over all of them put on love, the bond of perfection."},{"label":"Peace, Word and Thanksgiving (v. 15-17)","body":"Let Christ's peace rule as umpire, let His word dwell richly in the church through teaching and singing, and do everything in word or deed in the name of the Lord Jesus, giving thanks to the Father."},{"label":"The New Life at Home and Work (v. 18-25)","body":"Wives, husbands, children, fathers and servants are each addressed directly: submission, love without bitterness, obedience, fathers not discouraging children, and wholehearted work done for the Lord, who rewards and judges without partiality."}],"sections":[{"heading":"From Doctrine to Life: Where Christ Is","paragraphs":["Colossians 3 is the hinge of the letter. Chapters 1 and 2 have shown who Christ is — the image of the invisible God, the Creator, the Head of the church, the One in whom all fullness dwells, the conqueror of the powers. Now Paul shows what this means for the way believers live. But notice how he begins. He does not start with a rule; he starts with a fact: “If then you were raised together with Christ” (v. 1). In chapter 2, the false teachers offered rules: “don’t handle, nor taste, nor touch” (2:21). Paul said those rules “aren’t of any value against the indulgence of the flesh” (2:23). Here is the alternative. The power to live a holy life does not come from regulations; it comes from union with Christ in His death and resurrection.","Paul gives two commands built on that fact: “seek the things that are above” (v. 1) and “Set your mind on the things that are above” (v. 2). This is not escapism. Heavenly-minded Christians are not useless on earth; as the rest of the chapter shows, they make better spouses, parents and workers. ‘The things above’ are defined by Christ's presence: “where Christ is, seated on the right hand of God” (v. 1). To seek the things above is to orient everything — your desires, decisions, values and hopes — around the enthroned Jesus. As Jesus said, “for where your treasure is, there your heart will be also” (Matthew 6:21).","Then come the reasons. “For you died, and your life is hidden with Christ in God” (v. 3). Your old life ended at the cross. Your new life is secure and, for now, unseen. And it has a future: “When Christ, our life, is revealed, then you will also be revealed with him in glory” (v. 4). The Christian lives between two events — the resurrection of Christ, in which they already share, and the return of Christ, when their hidden life will be displayed. That is the frame for everything else in the chapter."]},{"heading":"Putting Off and Putting On","paragraphs":["Because you died with Christ, Paul says, “Put to death therefore your members which are on the earth” (v. 5). There seems to be a contradiction: if I died, why must I put anything to death? The answer is that Paul always moves from what is true in Christ to what must become true in practice. You died to sin's rule; now you must treat sin's desires as the leftovers of a dead life and refuse to feed them. The first list moves inward, from sexual immorality to the evil desire behind it, and ends with “covetousness, which is idolatry” (v. 5). Greed is idolatry because it puts something in God's place as the source of satisfaction and security.","The second list is about the sins of community: “anger, wrath, malice, slander, and shameful speaking out of your mouth” (v. 8), and then lying (v. 9). These are the sins that destroy a church from the inside. Paul's reason is again identity: “you have put off the old man with his doings, and have put on the new man” (vv. 9–10). And the new man is not a finished product but a process: “being renewed in knowledge after the image of his Creator” (v. 10). God is restoring in you the image He intended in Eden, and the pattern is Christ, the true image of God (1:15).","This renewed humanity has no room for the old divisions: “where there can’t be Greek and Jew, circumcision and uncircumcision, barbarian, Scythian, bondservant, freeman; but Christ is all, and in all” (v. 11). Paul lists the deepest racial, religious, cultural and social barriers of his world and declares them all irrelevant to standing in Christ. Then he shows the new wardrobe: “Put on therefore, as God’s chosen ones, holy and beloved, a heart of compassion, kindness, lowliness, humility, and perseverance” (v. 12). Every item is relational, and every item is a description of Jesus. The new self is not a new set of rules; it is Christ's own character being formed in His people, held together by love, “the bond of perfection” (v. 14)."]},{"heading":"Everything in His Name, Even at Home","paragraphs":["Verses 15–17 describe the church as it lives together under Christ. Three things fill it. First, peace: “let the peace of God rule in your hearts, to which also you were called in one body” (v. 15). Second, the word: “Let the word of Christ dwell in you richly” (v. 16), so that believers teach and admonish one another and sing together. Third, thanksgiving, which appears in each verse: “be thankful” (v. 15), “with grace in your heart” (v. 16), “giving thanks to God the Father, through him” (v. 17). The great summary is verse 17: “Whatever you do, in word or in deed, do all in the name of the Lord Jesus.” That sentence leaves no part of life outside Christ's lordship.","And so Paul goes straight into the home. The household codes of verses 18–4:1 can sound foreign to modern readers, and some have misused them. But read in their world they are radical. Paul addresses wives, children and slaves directly, as moral persons responsible before the Lord — not as property. Every instruction to the person with less power is matched by an instruction to the person with more: wives and husbands, children and fathers, servants and masters. And the husband's duty is not ‘rule your wife’ but “love your wives, and don’t be bitter against them” (v. 19). The father's duty is not ‘control your children’ but “don’t provoke your children, so that they won’t be discouraged” (v. 21).","The longest section is for servants, and it is full of dignity. Slaves were told to work “in singleness of heart, fearing God” (v. 22), and then: “And whatever you do, work heartily, as for the Lord, and not for men” (v. 23). Then the astonishing promise: “knowing that from the Lord you will receive the reward of the inheritance; for you serve the Lord Christ” (v. 24). People with no legal rights to inherit are told that they are heirs of God. And the warning of verse 25 — “there is no partiality” — reaches both ways, as 4:1 makes clear. In Christ, every task becomes worship, and every person, from the slave to the master, answers to the same Lord."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Twin Letter: Colossians and Ephesians","paragraphs":["Colossians 3 has a close twin in Ephesians 4–6, which Paul wrote at about the same time and sent with the same messenger, Tychicus. The ‘put off, put on’ language appears there too: “that you put away, as concerning your former way of life, the old man, that grows corrupt after the lusts of deceit” (Ephesians 4:22), and “put on the new man, who in the likeness of God has been created in righteousness and holiness of truth” (Ephesians 4:24). And the command to forgive is almost identical: “forgiving each other, just as God also in Christ forgave you” (Ephesians 4:32).","There is one striking difference. In Colossians, Paul says, “Let the word of Christ dwell in you richly” (v. 16), and the result is psalms, hymns and spiritual songs. In Ephesians, he says, “be filled with the Spirit” (Ephesians 5:18), and the result is the same: “speaking to one another in psalms, hymns, and spiritual songs; singing, and making melody in your heart to the Lord” (Ephesians 5:19). Being filled with the Spirit and being filled with the word of Christ produce the same fruit. The Spirit fills us through the word, and the word comes alive by the Spirit. The two cannot be separated.","The death-and-resurrection pattern of verses 1–4 is the same one Paul teaches in Romans 6: “We were buried therefore with him through baptism into death, that just as Christ was raised from the dead through the glory of the Father, so we also might walk in newness of life” (Romans 6:4). And the conclusion is the same: “Thus consider yourselves also to be dead to sin, but alive to God in Christ Jesus our Lord” (Romans 6:11). Galatians puts it personally: “I have been crucified with Christ, and it is no longer I that live, but Christ lives in me” (Galatians 2:20). Colossians 3 adds the future horizon: our hidden life will be revealed when Christ appears."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Hidden / concealed","hebrew":"κρύπτω","translit":"krýptō","strongs":"G2928","verses":"v. 3","insight":"Strong's: “to conceal (properly, by covering).” The perfect tense κέκρυπται means ‘has been hidden and remains hidden’. The same root gives us ‘cryptic’ and ‘crypt’. In 2:3 Paul used the related word ἀπόκρυφοι for the treasures of wisdom hidden in Christ. Now he says your own life is hidden in the same place. To be hidden is to be safe: like treasure locked in a vault, your life cannot be stolen. It is also to be unseen: the world, and sometimes even you, cannot fully see who you are in Christ. One day the hiding will end in revealing (v. 4)."},{"english":"Put to death / deaden","hebrew":"νεκρόω","translit":"nekróō","strongs":"G3499","verses":"v. 5","insight":"Strong's: “to deaden, i.e. (figuratively) to subdue.” The word is related to νεκρός, dead, from which we get ‘necro-’ words. Paul uses it in Romans 4:19 of Abraham's body being ‘as good as dead’. Here it is an aorist imperative: a decisive act. The believer does not negotiate with sin or manage it; they treat it as a corpse. This is not self-hatred or physical punishment, which Paul rejected in 2:23. It is refusing, by the power of the risen life, to give earthly desires any more room to live."},{"english":"Renew / renovate","hebrew":"ἀνακαινόω","translit":"anakainóō","strongs":"G341","verses":"v. 10","insight":"Strong's: “to renovate.” The word combines ἀνά, again, and καινός, new in quality. The present participle ἀνακαινούμενον means ‘being continually renewed’. The same word appears in 2 Corinthians 4:16, where the inner person is renewed day by day. The new self is real from the moment of conversion, yet it is still being renovated, like a house restored room by room. The goal is “the image of his Creator” — God remaking you to look like Jesus. Growth is not you becoming a better version of the old self; it is God finishing the new one."},{"english":"Rule as umpire / arbitrate","hebrew":"βραβεύω","translit":"brabeúō","strongs":"G1018","verses":"v. 15","insight":"Strong's: “to arbitrate, i.e. (genitive case) to govern (figuratively, prevail).” The word comes from βραβεύς, the umpire at the athletic games, and is related to βραβεῖον, the prize (Philippians 3:14). In 2:18 Paul used a compound of the same word: καταβραβευέτω, ‘let no one disqualify you’ — the false teachers wanted to be the umpires of the Colossians' spiritual lives. Here Paul says the true umpire is Christ's peace. Not human judges, but His peace decides whether you are on course."},{"english":"Dwell in / take up residence","hebrew":"ἐνοικέω","translit":"enoikéō","strongs":"G1774","verses":"v. 16","insight":"Strong's: “to inhabit (figuratively).” The word combines ἐν, in, and οἰκέω, to dwell in a house (οἶκος). It is used of the Holy Spirit dwelling in believers (Romans 8:11; 2 Timothy 1:14) and of faith dwelling in Timothy's family (2 Timothy 1:5). Paul wants the word of Christ to live in the church as in its own home, not as a visitor. And it is to dwell πλουσίως, richly — filling every room, overflowing into conversation and song. A church where the word dwells richly will teach, warn, comfort and sing."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: where your mind lives, what you are still wearing, and who you are really working for.","items":[{"title":"Where Is Your Mind?","body":"The Spirit asks you a simple question: what do you think about when you are not forced to think about anything? Paul says, “Set your mind on the things that are above, not on the things that are on the earth” (v. 2). Your thought-life follows your treasure. If your mind keeps returning to money, status, a grievance, a fear or a desire, the Spirit is showing you what has taken the throne. He does not condemn you for it; He invites you to lift your eyes to the One “seated on the right hand of God” (v. 1). Every time your mind drifts, bring it back to Jesus. That is not escape from life; it is the beginning of a new one."},{"title":"Old Clothes in the Wardrobe","body":"Some believers have put off the old self in principle but still keep a few old garments in the wardrobe, just in case: a familiar anger, a habit of slander, a small lie that makes life easier, a secret desire. The Spirit is gently opening that wardrobe. “But now you also put them all away” (v. 8) — all of them. He knows which garment you reach for under pressure. Let Him name it today, and give it up. Then let Him clothe you with what belongs to Christ: compassion, kindness, lowliness, gentleness and patience, and over all of it, love."},{"title":"You Serve the Lord Christ","body":"Many believers feel their daily work is unspiritual — a job they endure between the things that really matter. The Spirit wants to change that. “And whatever you do, work heartily, as for the Lord, and not for men” (v. 23). Your boss is not your final audience; your customers, your family, even your church leaders are not your final audience. “You serve the Lord Christ” (v. 24). When you are overlooked, underpaid or unthanked, He sees. When no one is watching, He is. Let the Spirit turn your ordinary work into worship, and your workplace into a place where Christ is served."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Mind Check","body":"For one day, notice where your mind goes when it is free — in the shower, in traffic, before sleep. Write down the three things it returns to most. Then ask: are these things above or things on the earth? Choose one verse about Christ's reign (v. 1) to bring your mind back each time it drifts."},{"title":"The Wardrobe Check","body":"Read the lists in verses 5, 8 and 9 slowly. Circle the one sin that you most often ‘wear’ under pressure. Then read verse 12 and circle the garment that is its opposite. Ask God to help you take off the first and put on the second this week, in one specific relationship."},{"title":"The Forgiveness Check","body":"Is there someone you have a complaint against (v. 13)? Write their name down. Then write next to it what Christ has forgiven you. Ask whether you are measuring their debt by your own standard or by His. Decide today to forgive, even if reconciliation takes longer."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"The Word at Home","body":"Take verse 16 literally this week: let the word of Christ dwell in your home. Choose one short passage — Colossians 3:1–4 or 3:12–17 is ideal — and read it aloud each day with your household or, if you live alone, with a friend by phone. Talk about one phrase. Sing a hymn or worship song that carries its message. Let the word become part of the conversation of your home, not just of your Sunday. At the end of the week, write down how it changed the atmosphere and the way you spoke to each other."},{"title":"The ‘In His Name’ Day","body":"Pick one ordinary working day and treat every task as done “in the name of the Lord Jesus” (v. 17). Before each piece of work, pause for a few seconds and say, ‘Lord, this is for You.’ Work with the same care whether or not anyone is watching (v. 22). Give thanks at each meal and each finished task. In the evening, write down what was different: your attitude, your quality of work, your patience with others. Repeat it until it stops being an experiment and becomes your way of life."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, You are seated at the right hand of God, and I have been raised with You. Lift my eyes from the things on the earth and set my mind on the things above, where You are. Thank You that I died with You and that my life is hidden with You in God, safe beyond the reach of every enemy. When You appear, I will appear with You in glory; let that hope shape how I live today. By Your power I put to death what belongs to my old life: impurity, evil desire and the greed that is idolatry. I put away anger, malice, slander and every lie. Clothe me with the new self, renewed after Your image. Make me compassionate, kind, humble, gentle and patient. Teach me to bear with others and to forgive as You forgave me. Over everything, wrap me in love. Let Your peace rule in my heart and in Your church, let Your word dwell in me richly, and fill my mouth with thanksgiving and song. In my home, make me loving and not bitter. In my work, let me serve You heartily, knowing that I serve the Lord Christ. Whatever I do, in word or deed, let me do it all in Your name, giving thanks to the Father through You. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Servant Who Would Not Forgive","ref":"Matthew 18:21-35","relatedVerses":"v. 13","summary":"Peter asked Jesus how often he should forgive a brother who sinned against him — up to seven times? Jesus said, “I don’t tell you until seven times, but, until seventy times seven” (Matthew 18:22), and told a story. A king settled accounts with a servant who owed him ten thousand talents, a debt no one could ever repay. When the servant begged for patience, the king was “moved with compassion, released him, and forgave him the debt” (Matthew 18:27). But that same servant went out, found a fellow servant who owed him a hundred denarii, seized him by the throat and threw him into prison. The king called him back and said, “Shouldn’t you also have had mercy on your fellow servant, even as I had mercy on you?” (Matthew 18:33). The parable is the story behind Paul's words, “even as Christ forgave you, so you also do” (v. 13). Those who have been forgiven an unpayable debt cannot hold small debts over others."},{"title":"Singing at Midnight in Philippi","ref":"Acts 16:22-34","relatedVerses":"v. 15-17","summary":"In Philippi, Paul and Silas were stripped, beaten with rods and thrown into the inner prison with their feet in the stocks. Their circumstances gave them every reason for bitterness. Instead, “about midnight Paul and Silas were praying and singing hymns to God, and the prisoners were listening to them” (Acts 16:25). An earthquake opened the doors and loosened every chain. The terrified jailer was about to kill himself, but Paul stopped him, and that night the jailer and his household believed and were baptized. This is Colossians 3:15–17 lived out: the peace of Christ ruling in a dark place, the word of Christ overflowing in song, and thanksgiving that does not depend on circumstances. The prisoners listened, and a household was saved."},{"title":"Joseph in Potiphar's House","ref":"Genesis 39:1-23","relatedVerses":"v. 22-25","summary":"Joseph was sold by his brothers and bought as a slave by Potiphar, an Egyptian officer. He had every reason to do the minimum. Instead, he worked so faithfully that the Lord was with him and blessed Potiphar's house for his sake, and Potiphar put everything he owned in Joseph's care. When Potiphar's wife tried to seduce him day after day, Joseph refused: how could he do this great wickedness and sin against God? He was falsely accused and thrown into prison, yet even there the Lord was with him, and the jailer trusted him with everything. Joseph worked in singleness of heart, fearing God, whether anyone was watching or not. Centuries before Paul wrote it, Joseph lived out the truth of verse 23: work heartily, as for the Lord and not for men. His real Master saw, and in time lifted him to the right hand of Pharaoh."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Colossians","chapter":4,"chapterTitle":"Prayer, Wise Speech and Faithful Friends — The Gospel Carried by Ordinary People","themeTitle":"“Take Heed to the Ministry Which You Have Received in the Lord”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Masters, give to your servants that which is just and equal, knowing that you also have a Master in heaven.","understanding":"This verse completes the household code of chapter 3. κύριοι, masters, must give their δοῦλοι, servants, “that which is just and equal.” τὸ δίκαιον is what is right; τὴν ἰσότητα is fairness, equity — a striking word to use of slaves, who in Roman law had no rights at all. The reason: “you also have a Master in heaven.” The same word κύριος is used for the earthly master and the heavenly one. Every earthly master is himself a servant, and will answer to his Lord for the way he treated those under him.","words":[{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"masters"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"servants"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"just"},{"strongs":"G2471","hebrew":"ἰσότης","translit":"isótēs","english":"equal"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"}]},{"v":2,"text":"Continue steadfastly in prayer, watching therein with thanksgiving;","understanding":"τῇ προσευχῇ προσκαρτερεῖτε, “Continue steadfastly in prayer.” The verb means to persist, to be devoted to, to keep at something without giving up. It describes the early church in Acts 1:14 and 2:42. γρηγοροῦντες, watching, staying awake and alert — the word Jesus used in Gethsemane when His disciples fell asleep. And all of it ἐν εὐχαριστίᾳ, with thanksgiving. Prayer that is persistent, alert and grateful is the engine room of the church.","words":[{"strongs":"G4335","hebrew":"προσευχή","translit":"proseuchḗ","english":"prayer"},{"strongs":"G4342","hebrew":"προσκαρτερέω","translit":"proskarteréō","english":"continue steadfastly"},{"strongs":"G1127","hebrew":"γρηγορεύω","translit":"grēgoreúō","english":"watching"},{"strongs":"G2169","hebrew":"εὐχαριστία","translit":"eucharistía","english":"thanksgiving"}]},{"v":3,"text":"praying together for us also, that God may open to us a door for the word, to speak the mystery of Christ, for which I am also in bonds;","understanding":"Paul asks them to pray “for us also.” He does not ask for release from prison, but that God would ἀνοίξῃ θύραν τοῦ λόγου, open a door for the word. The open door is Paul's picture of opportunity for the gospel (1 Corinthians 16:9; 2 Corinthians 2:12). The content is “the mystery of Christ” (μυστήριον), the secret once hidden and now revealed: Christ in you, the hope of glory (1:27). And the setting: δέδεμαι, “I am also in bonds” — chained. His chains have not closed the door; prayer can still open it.","words":[{"strongs":"G4336","hebrew":"προσεύχομαι","translit":"proseúchomai","english":"praying"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"open"},{"strongs":"G2374","hebrew":"θύρα","translit":"thýra","english":"door"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G3466","hebrew":"μυστήριον","translit":"mystḗrion","english":"mystery"},{"strongs":"G1210","hebrew":"δέω","translit":"déō","english":"in bonds"}]},{"v":4,"text":"that I may reveal it as I ought to speak.","understanding":"“That I may reveal it as I ought to speak.” φανερώσω, I may make it clear, visible, plain. The same verb was used of Christ being revealed in 3:4. Paul wants to preach with clarity. ὡς δεῖ με λαλῆσαι, as it is necessary for me to speak: he feels the obligation of a messenger who must deliver the message faithfully. Even the greatest apostle asks for prayer that he would speak clearly.","words":[{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"reveal"},{"strongs":"G1163","hebrew":"δεῖ","translit":"deî","english":"ought"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"speak"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Walk in wisdom toward those who are outside, redeeming the time.","understanding":"Now Paul turns from prayer for the preacher to the witness of the whole church. ἐν σοφίᾳ περιπατεῖτε, walk in wisdom, toward τοὺς ἔξω, “those who are outside” — unbelievers watching the church. ἐξαγοραζόμενοι τὸν καιρόν, “redeeming the time” — literally buying up the opportunity, like a merchant snapping up a bargain in the market. καιρός is not clock-time but the right moment. Every encounter with an unbeliever is an opportunity to be bought up, not wasted.","words":[{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G4043","hebrew":"περιπατέω","translit":"peripatéō","english":"walk"},{"strongs":"G1854","hebrew":"ἔξω","translit":"éxō","english":"outside"},{"strongs":"G1805","hebrew":"ἐξαγοράζω","translit":"exagorázō","english":"redeeming"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"time"}]},{"v":6,"text":"Let your speech always be with grace, seasoned with salt, that you may know how you ought to answer each one.","understanding":"“Let your speech always be with grace” — ἐν χάριτι, gracious, kind, attractive, full of the grace you received. “Seasoned with salt” — ἅλατι ἠρτυμένος. Salt gives flavour and preserves from decay: Christian speech should be interesting rather than dull, and wholesome rather than corrupt. The purpose: “that you may know how you ought to answer each one.” ἀποκρίνεσθαι, to answer: each person is different, and wise believers do not give everyone the same speech. Compare 1 Peter 3:15: “always be ready to give an answer to everyone who asks you a reason concerning the hope that is in you.”","words":[{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"speech"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G217","hebrew":"ἅλας","translit":"hálas","english":"salt"},{"strongs":"G741","hebrew":"ἀρτύω","translit":"artýō","english":"seasoned"},{"strongs":"G611","hebrew":"ἀποκρίνομαι","translit":"apokrínomai","english":"answer"}]},{"v":7,"text":"All my affairs will be made known to you by Tychicus, the beloved brother, faithful servant, and fellow bondservant in the Lord.","understanding":"Τύχικος, Tychicus, from the province of Asia (Acts 20:4), carried this letter and the letter to the Ephesians. Paul gives him three titles: ἀγαπητὸς ἀδελφός, “the beloved brother”; πιστὸς διάκονος, “faithful servant”; and σύνδουλος, “fellow bondservant in the Lord.” He is loved, trustworthy and a partner in service. Paul's ministry depended on people like Tychicus: couriers who walked hundreds of miles carrying letters that would become Scripture.","words":[{"strongs":"G5190","hebrew":"Τυχικός","translit":"Tychikós","english":"Tychicus"},{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G1249","hebrew":"διάκονος","translit":"diákonos","english":"servant"},{"strongs":"G4889","hebrew":"σύνδουλος","translit":"sýndoulos","english":"fellow bondservant"}]},{"v":8,"text":"I am sending him to you for this very purpose, that he may know your circumstances and comfort your hearts,","understanding":"ἔπεμψα, I am sending — the ‘epistolary aorist’, written from the readers' point of view when the letter arrives. Tychicus has two jobs: to learn their circumstances and to παρακαλέσῃ τὰς καρδίας ὑμῶν, comfort, encourage, strengthen “your hearts.” The same phrase appears in 2:2. Ministry is not only information; it is encouragement carried in person.","words":[{"strongs":"G3992","hebrew":"πέμπω","translit":"pémpō","english":"sending"},{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"comfort"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"hearts"}]},{"v":9,"text":"together with Onesimus, the faithful and beloved brother, who is one of you. They will make known to you everything that is going on here.","understanding":"“Together with Onesimus, the faithful and beloved brother, who is one of you.” Ὀνήσιμος means ‘useful’. He was a slave of Philemon in Colossae who had run away, met Paul in prison and been converted. Now he is coming home. Paul does not call him ‘the runaway slave’ but πιστὸς καὶ ἀγαπητὸς ἀδελφός, the faithful and beloved brother — the same titles given to Tychicus. And “one of you”: he belongs to the Colossian church now, not merely to Philemon's household. Colossians 3:11 is happening in real life.","words":[{"strongs":"G3682","hebrew":"Ὀνήσιμος","translit":"Onḗsimos","english":"Onesimus"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"}]},{"v":10,"text":"Aristarchus, my fellow prisoner, greets you, and Mark, the cousin of Barnabas (concerning whom you received commandments, “if he comes to you, receive him”),","understanding":"Ἀρίσταρχος, Aristarchus, a Macedonian from Thessalonica (Acts 27:2), had been dragged into the theatre during the Ephesus riot and sailed with Paul to Rome. Here he is συναιχμάλωτος, “my fellow prisoner” — literally a fellow prisoner of war. Then Μᾶρκος, Mark, ἀνεψιός, “the cousin of Barnabas.” This is the same Mark who had deserted Paul on the first missionary journey and caused the split between Paul and Barnabas (Acts 15:37–39). Now Paul commends him: “if he comes to you, receive him” (δέξασθε). Failure is not final in God's kingdom.","words":[{"strongs":"G708","hebrew":"Ἀρίσταρχος","translit":"Arístarchos","english":"Aristarchus"},{"strongs":"G4869","hebrew":"συναιχμάλωτος","translit":"synaichmálōtos","english":"fellow prisoner"},{"strongs":"G3138","hebrew":"Μάρκος","translit":"Márkos","english":"Mark"},{"strongs":"G431","hebrew":"ἀνεψιός","translit":"anepsiós","english":"cousin"},{"strongs":"G921","hebrew":"Βαρνάβας","translit":"Barnábas","english":"Barnabas"},{"strongs":"G1209","hebrew":"δέχομαι","translit":"déchomai","english":"receive"}]},{"v":11,"text":"and Jesus who is called Justus. These are my only fellow workers for God’s Kingdom who are of the circumcision, men who have been a comfort to me.","understanding":"Ἰησοῦς ὁ λεγόμενος Ἰοῦστος, “Jesus who is called Justus” — Jesus (Joshua) was a common Jewish name, and he used the Latin name Justus, ‘righteous’. These three, Aristarchus, Mark and Justus, are Paul's only συνεργοί, “fellow workers for God’s Kingdom who are of the circumcision” — Jewish believers. Many of Paul's fellow Jews had opposed him, so these men were precious. They had been παρηγορία, “a comfort to me.” The word is the root of the English ‘paregoric’, a soothing medicine.","words":[{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"},{"strongs":"G2459","hebrew":"Ἰοῦστος","translit":"Ioûstos","english":"Justus"},{"strongs":"G4904","hebrew":"συνεργός","translit":"synergós","english":"fellow workers"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"Kingdom"},{"strongs":"G4061","hebrew":"περιτομή","translit":"peritomḗ","english":"circumcision"},{"strongs":"G3931","hebrew":"παρηγορία","translit":"parēgoría","english":"comfort"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Epaphras, who is one of you, a servant of Christ, salutes you, always striving for you in his prayers, that you may stand perfect and complete in all the will of God.","understanding":"Ἐπαφρᾶς, Epaphras, founded the church in Colossae (1:7) and is “one of you.” He is now with Paul in Rome, and his ministry continues in prayer: “always striving for you in his prayers.” ἀγωνιζόμενος, striving, agonising, wrestling like an athlete in the games — the same word as Paul's ‘striving’ in 1:29 and the related ἀγών of 2:1. His request: that they “stand perfect and complete in all the will of God.” τέλειοι, mature; πεπληροφορημένοι, fully assured, filled up. The pastor far away is fighting for his people on his knees.","words":[{"strongs":"G1889","hebrew":"Ἐπαφρᾶς","translit":"Epaphrâs","english":"Epaphras"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"servant"},{"strongs":"G75","hebrew":"ἀγωνίζομαι","translit":"agōnízomai","english":"striving"},{"strongs":"G5046","hebrew":"τέλειος","translit":"téleios","english":"perfect"},{"strongs":"G4135","hebrew":"πληροφορέω","translit":"plērophoréō","english":"complete"},{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"}]},{"v":13,"text":"For I testify about him, that he has great zeal for you, and for those in Laodicea, and for those in Hierapolis.","understanding":"Paul adds his own witness: μαρτυρῶ, “I testify about him, that he has great zeal for you.” ζῆλος is burning intensity. His concern extends to the three churches of the Lycus valley: Colossae, Λαοδίκεια, Laodicea, ten miles away, and Ἱεράπολις, Hierapolis, six miles beyond. Epaphras probably planted all three. Jesus later warned the Laodiceans about their lukewarmness (Revelation 3:15–16); perhaps they needed Epaphras's prayers more than anyone.","words":[{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"testify"},{"strongs":"G2205","hebrew":"ζῆλος","translit":"zēlos","english":"zeal"},{"strongs":"G2993","hebrew":"Λαοδίκεια","translit":"Laodíkeia","english":"Laodicea"},{"strongs":"G2404","hebrew":"Ἱεράπολις","translit":"Hierápolis","english":"Hierapolis"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Luke, the beloved physician, and Demas greet you.","understanding":"Two very different names stand side by side. Λουκᾶς, Luke, ὁ ἰατρὸς ὁ ἀγαπητός, “the beloved physician,” author of the Gospel of Luke and the book of Acts, who stayed with Paul to the very end. And Δημᾶς, Demas, mentioned without any description. A few years later, Paul wrote, “for Demas left me, having loved this present world” (2 Timothy 4:10), and in the same breath, “Only Luke is with me” (2 Timothy 4:11). Two men served side by side; one finished, one fell away. This verse is a quiet warning.","words":[{"strongs":"G3065","hebrew":"Λουκᾶς","translit":"Loukâs","english":"Luke"},{"strongs":"G2395","hebrew":"ἰατρός","translit":"iatrós","english":"physician"},{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G1214","hebrew":"Δημᾶς","translit":"Dēmâs","english":"Demas"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Greet the brothers who are in Laodicea, and Nymphas, and the assembly that is in his house.","understanding":"ἀσπάσασθε, greet, the brothers in Laodicea, and Νυμφᾶς, Nymphas (some manuscripts read Nympha, a woman), “and the assembly that is in his house.” The early church had no buildings; ἐκκλησία, the assembly, met in homes. A believer who opened their house became the host of a congregation. Here is how the gospel spread in the first century: through households.","words":[{"strongs":"G782","hebrew":"ἀσπάζομαι","translit":"aspázomai","english":"greet"},{"strongs":"G3564","hebrew":"Νυμφᾶς","translit":"Nymphâs","english":"Nymphas"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"house"}]},{"v":16,"text":"When this letter has been read among you, cause it to be read also in the assembly of the Laodiceans; and that you also read the letter from Laodicea.","understanding":"“When this letter has been read among you” — ἀναγνωσθῇ, read aloud publicly, because most believers could not read and copies were rare. Then Paul tells them to send it to Laodicea, and to read ‘the letter from Laodicea’ in return. Paul's letters were meant to circulate among the churches. The letter from Laodicea may be lost, or it may be the letter we call Ephesians, which seems to have been a circular letter. Either way, the church was learning to receive apostolic letters as the word of God.","words":[{"strongs":"G1992","hebrew":"ἐπιστολή","translit":"epistolḗ","english":"letter"},{"strongs":"G314","hebrew":"ἀναγινώσκω","translit":"anaginṓskō","english":"read"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"},{"strongs":"G2994","hebrew":"Λαοδικεύς","translit":"Laodikeús","english":"Laodiceans"}]},{"v":17,"text":"Tell Archippus, “Take heed to the ministry which you have received in the Lord, that you fulfill it.”","understanding":"A personal message for Ἄρχιππος, Archippus, whom Philemon 1:2 calls a ‘fellow soldier’ and who may have been Philemon's son. βλέπε, “Take heed,” look carefully, to the διακονία, the ministry, “which you have received in the Lord, that you fulfill it” (πληροῖς, fill it up, complete it). Paul does not say what the ministry was. Perhaps Archippus was leading the church in Epaphras's absence and needed encouragement. The message reaches every believer: you have received a ministry from the Lord. Finish it.","words":[{"strongs":"G751","hebrew":"Ἄρχιππος","translit":"Árchippos","english":"Archippus"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"take heed"},{"strongs":"G1248","hebrew":"διακονία","translit":"diakonía","english":"ministry"},{"strongs":"G4137","hebrew":"πληρόω","translit":"plēróō","english":"fulfill"}]},{"v":18,"text":"The salutation of me, Paul, with my own hand: remember my bonds. Grace be with you. Amen.","understanding":"Paul took the pen from his secretary and wrote the last lines himself: ὁ ἀσπασμὸς τῇ ἐμῇ χειρί, “The salutation of me, Paul, with my own hand” — the mark of authenticity in his letters (2 Thessalonians 3:17). Then three short words: μνημονεύετέ μου τῶν δεσμῶν, “remember my bonds.” You can almost hear the chain rattling against the desk as he writes. And the last word of the letter is the first word of the gospel: “Grace be with you.”","words":[{"strongs":"G783","hebrew":"ἀσπασμός","translit":"aspasmós","english":"salutation"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"},{"strongs":"G3421","hebrew":"μνημονεύω","translit":"mnēmoneúō","english":"remember"},{"strongs":"G1199","hebrew":"δεσμόν","translit":"desmón","english":"bonds"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Chained Man and the Open Door","verseRef":"v. 2-4","image":"Picture a small room in Rome. A man sits on a bench with an iron chain running from his wrist to the arm of a Roman soldier. The door of the room is locked from the outside. He cannot leave. But he is not praying for the lock to break. He is dictating a letter, and in it he asks his friends hundreds of miles away to pray that God would open a different door — a door for the word. Now picture that prayer rising from a house church in Colossae, from tired slaves and busy mothers and old men on their knees. And picture doors opening: the soldier chained to Paul hears the gospel, then the next soldier, then the household of Caesar. The chain stays on, but the word runs free.","meaning":"Paul asks, “that God may open to us a door for the word, to speak the mystery of Christ, for which I am also in bonds” (v. 3). His priority was not his own comfort but the progress of the gospel. In Philippians he reported that his bonds in Christ had become “evident to the whole palace guard” (Philippians 1:13). Later he would write, “But God’s word isn’t chained” (2 Timothy 2:9). The open door is God's work, but it comes through the prayers of ordinary believers. Your prayers for missionaries, pastors and believers in hard places can open doors that no human power can shut."},{"title":"Salt on the Table","verseRef":"v. 5-6","image":"Picture a plain meal set before a hungry traveller: bread, beans, a piece of fish. It is nourishing but flat. Then someone reaches for a small bowl of salt and sprinkles it over the food. Suddenly the flavour comes alive. Now picture a conversation at a workplace, a market stall or a neighbour's door. Someone who does not know Christ asks a question, perhaps sarcastically, perhaps sincerely. One believer answers harshly, another nervously, another with a prepared speech. But one believer answers with grace, warmth and a little salt — honest, interesting, kind, fitted to that person — and the listener leans in, wanting to hear more.","meaning":"Paul says, “Let your speech always be with grace, seasoned with salt, that you may know how you ought to answer each one” (v. 6). Grace makes our speech kind; salt makes it interesting and wholesome. Christians should not be bland, and they should not be bitter. And they must be wise: “Walk in wisdom toward those who are outside, redeeming the time” (v. 5). Not every person needs the same words. Paying attention to the person in front of you, and speaking from the grace you have received, is how ordinary conversations become doors for the gospel."},{"title":"The Second Chance on the Road","verseRef":"v. 10","image":"Picture a young man on a dusty road in Pamphylia, turning back. The mission ahead is hard and dangerous, and he is going home to Jerusalem. Years later, picture an argument between two great missionaries over whether to take him with them again: one says no, the other says yes, and they part ways. Then picture, years after that, an old apostle in chains writing a letter and pausing to add a line: ‘If Mark comes to you, receive him.’ And later still, near the end of his life, the same apostle asking for him by name: ‘Bring Mark; he is useful to me.’ The young man who turned back is welcomed home to the work.","meaning":"Mark's story is one of the most encouraging in the New Testament. Paul refused to take him on the second journey “someone who had withdrawn from them in Pamphylia, and didn’t go with them to do the work” (Acts 15:38). But Barnabas did not give up on him, and neither did God. In Colossians 4:10 Paul commends him, and in 2 Timothy he writes, “Take Mark, and bring him with you, for he is useful to me for service” (2 Timothy 4:11). Mark went on to write the second Gospel. Your past failure is not the end of your story. And if someone has failed you, ask whether God may be restoring them too."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Masters and the Master in Heaven (v. 1)","body":"The household code closes: masters must treat servants justly and fairly, remembering that they too have a Master in heaven."},{"label":"Prayer and Witness (v. 2-6)","body":"Continue steadfastly in watchful, thankful prayer; pray for an open door for the word for the imprisoned Paul; walk wisely toward outsiders, making the most of every opportunity, with speech full of grace and seasoned with salt."},{"label":"The Messengers (v. 7-9)","body":"Tychicus, the beloved brother and faithful servant, is sent to bring news and comfort their hearts, together with Onesimus, the faithful and beloved brother, who is one of them."},{"label":"Greetings From Paul's Companions (v. 10-14)","body":"Aristarchus, Mark and Justus — his only Jewish fellow workers — send greetings, as do Epaphras, who wrestles in prayer for the Lycus valley churches, and Luke and Demas."},{"label":"Final Instructions and Signature (v. 15-18)","body":"Greet Laodicea and the church in Nymphas's house; exchange letters with Laodicea; tell Archippus to fulfil his ministry; and Paul signs with his own hand, asking them to remember his chains."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Prayer That Opens Doors","paragraphs":["The household code ends with a word to the powerful: “Masters, give to your servants that which is just and equal, knowing that you also have a Master in heaven” (v. 1). The word ‘equal’ (ἰσότης) was shocking. No Roman law required masters to treat slaves fairly. But Paul places every master under a higher Master, and in doing so plants a seed that would one day grow into the end of slavery among Christian peoples. The final word on human authority is that it is accountable to Christ.","Then Paul turns to prayer. “Continue steadfastly in prayer, watching therein with thanksgiving” (v. 2). The three qualities belong together. Steadfast prayer does not give up after one request. Watchful prayer stays alert — to temptation, to the needs of others, to what God is doing. Thankful prayer remembers God's past faithfulness and so keeps hope alive. The letter began with Paul's own prayers for the Colossians (1:3, 9); now it ends by asking them to pray for him.","And what does he ask for? Not freedom, comfort or safety, but an open door: “that God may open to us a door for the word, to speak the mystery of Christ, for which I am also in bonds; that I may reveal it as I ought to speak” (vv. 3–4). The mystery of Christ is the whole message of the letter: God's eternal plan, hidden for ages, now revealed — Christ in you, the hope of glory. Paul was chained because of that message, and he wanted to keep preaching it clearly. His request shows that the gospel's progress depends not only on preachers but on the prayers of the church."]},{"heading":"Wisdom and Words for the Watching World","paragraphs":["Not every Colossian could preach like Paul, but every one of them could live and speak wisely before unbelieving neighbours. “Walk in wisdom toward those who are outside, redeeming the time” (v. 5). The false teachers had offered a secret wisdom for insiders; Paul's wisdom is practical and turned outward. The church lives in public. Its behaviour either opens or closes doors for the gospel. And opportunities are not endless: ‘redeeming the time’ means seizing each moment the Lord gives, because it may not come again.","Then comes a beautiful description of Christian speech: “Let your speech always be with grace, seasoned with salt, that you may know how you ought to answer each one” (v. 6). Grace is the foundation: speech that reflects the grace we have received — kind, generous, never cruel. Salt adds flavour and preserves: our words should be real, thoughtful and wholesome, neither dull nor corrupt. And wisdom shapes it: ‘each one’ means that we answer people as individuals, listening before we speak. The same truth is presented differently to a grieving widow, a sceptical student and a hostile neighbour.","This connects with the vices of chapter 3. Believers are to put away “anger, wrath, malice, slander, and shameful speaking out of your mouth” (3:8), and to let “the word of Christ dwell in you richly” (3:16). A mouth emptied of slander and filled with the word of Christ will speak with grace to the watching world. Evangelism in Colossians is not a technique; it is the overflow of a transformed life."]},{"heading":"The People Behind the Letter","paragraphs":["The final section is a list of names, and it is easy to skip. But these names show how the gospel actually spread: through a network of ordinary, faithful, flawed people. Tychicus, the courier, carried the letter and the news (vv. 7–8). Onesimus, the runaway slave turned brother, came home with him (v. 9). Aristarchus shared Paul's imprisonment (v. 10). Mark, once a deserter, was now a trusted worker (v. 10). Justus, a Jewish believer, stood by Paul when many Jews opposed him (v. 11). Epaphras wrestled in prayer (v. 12). Luke, the physician, stayed faithful to the end (v. 14). Nymphas opened a home for the church (v. 15). Archippus had a ministry to finish (v. 17).","There are warnings here too. Demas is mentioned with no description (v. 14). Years later he would abandon Paul, “having loved this present world” (2 Timothy 4:10). Being near great ministry is not the same as finishing faithfully. And Archippus receives a public charge: “Take heed to the ministry which you have received in the Lord, that you fulfill it” (v. 17). Every believer has received something from the Lord to do. Starting is good; fulfilling is the goal.","The letter ends with Paul taking the pen in his own chained hand: “The salutation of me, Paul, with my own hand: remember my bonds. Grace be with you. Amen” (v. 18). It is a moving close. The letter that proclaimed Christ as Creator, Head and Conqueror of all powers was written by a prisoner. Its final word is grace — the word that opened the letter (1:2). From beginning to end, the Christian life is grace: received in Christ, lived in Christ, shared with others in Christ."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Faces From Acts and the Later Letters","paragraphs":["Many of the names in Colossians 4 appear elsewhere in the New Testament, and their stories add depth to this chapter. Tychicus is introduced in Acts as one of Paul's travelling companions: “Tychicus and Trophimus of Asia” (Acts 20:4). In Ephesians, Paul describes him in nearly the same words as here: “Tychicus, the beloved brother and faithful servant in the Lord, will make known to you all things” (Ephesians 6:21), and adds, “whom I have sent to you for this very purpose, that you may know our state, and that he may comfort your hearts” (Ephesians 6:22). The two letters travelled together.","Mark's story begins in Jerusalem, where the church prayed for Peter in “the house of Mary, the mother of John who was called Mark” (Acts 12:12). Then came his failure and the division: “Barnabas planned to take John, who was called Mark, with them also” (Acts 15:37), and “the contention grew so sharp that they separated from each other” (Acts 15:39). Colossians 4:10 shows the reconciliation, and 2 Timothy shows it was complete: “Take Mark, and bring him with you, for he is useful to me for service” (2 Timothy 4:11).","The prayer request of verse 3 matches Ephesians 6:19, where Paul asks for prayer “that utterance may be given to me in opening my mouth, to make known with boldness the mystery of the Good News” (Ephesians 6:19), and he adds, “for which I am an ambassador in chains” (Ephesians 6:20). And the companion letter to Philemon, carried in the same bag, tells Onesimus's story in full: Paul sends him back to his master no longer as a slave but as a beloved brother. Colossians 4:9 is the public side of that private appeal."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Continue steadfastly / persevere","hebrew":"προσκαρτερέω","translit":"proskarteréō","strongs":"G4342","verses":"v. 2","insight":"Strong's: “to be earnest towards, i.e. (to a thing) to persevere, be constantly diligent.” The word combines πρός, towards, and καρτερέω, to be strong or to endure. It describes the first believers, who “continued steadfastly in the apostles’ teaching and fellowship, in the breaking of bread, and prayer” (Acts 2:42), and Romans 12:12 uses it of being ‘constant in prayer’. It is not a burst of prayer in a crisis but a stubborn, settled devotion. Prayer is a habit of the strong-hearted, who keep coming back to God when answers are slow."},{"english":"Redeem / buy up","hebrew":"ἐξαγοράζω","translit":"exagorázō","strongs":"G1805","verses":"v. 5","insight":"Strong's: “to buy up, i.e. ransom; figuratively, to rescue from loss (improve opportunity).” The word combines ἐκ, out of, and ἀγοράζω, to buy in the market (ἀγορά). In Galatians 3:13 and 4:5 it is used of Christ redeeming us from the curse of the law. Here it is used of time: buy up the καιρός, the opportunity, before it is lost. A merchant who sees a rare bargain does not wait until tomorrow. Believers are to treat every conversation with an outsider as precious and limited."},{"english":"Salt","hebrew":"ἅλας","translit":"hálas","strongs":"G217","verses":"v. 6","insight":"Strong's: “salt; figuratively, prudence.” Salt was essential in the ancient world for preserving food and giving it flavour; it was also used in sacrifices and covenants. Jesus said, “You are the salt of the earth” (Matthew 5:13) and, “Have salt in yourselves, and be at peace with one another” (Mark 9:50). Speech seasoned with salt is wholesome rather than corrupt, interesting rather than bland, and wise rather than careless. Too little salt is tasteless; too much is unpleasant. Wise speech knows the right measure for each person."},{"english":"Strive / agonise / wrestle","hebrew":"ἀγωνίζομαι","translit":"agōnízomai","strongs":"G75","verses":"v. 12","insight":"Strong's: “to struggle, literally (to compete for a prize), figuratively (to contend with an adversary), or genitive case (to endeavor to accomplish something).” It is the root of our word ‘agonise’. Paul uses it of himself in 1:29 — “striving according to his working” — and of the good fight of faith in 1 Timothy 6:12. Here it describes Epaphras's prayers. Prayer is not passive. It is a contest, a wrestling for people's spiritual growth against everything that would hold them back. Epaphras could not be in Colossae, but he could fight for them on his knees."},{"english":"Ministry / service","hebrew":"διακονία","translit":"diakonía","strongs":"G1248","verses":"v. 17","insight":"Strong's: “attendance (as a servant, etc.); figuratively (eleemosynary) aid, (official) service.” From διάκονος, servant, the word originally described waiting at tables. In Acts 6:1–4 it is used both for serving food to widows and for the ministry of the word. All ministry is service. Archippus is told to take heed to the ministry ‘received in the Lord’: it is a gift and a trust from Christ, not a career or a title. And it must be fulfilled, brought to completion. Every believer has a διακονία to discover and to finish."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the faithfulness of your prayers, the flavour of your words, and the finishing of your ministry.","items":[{"title":"The Prayer That Keeps Going","body":"Many believers pray in bursts: when there is a crisis, or after a powerful meeting. The Spirit is calling you to “Continue steadfastly in prayer” (v. 2). Who have you stopped praying for because the answer was slow? Which missionary, pastor or persecuted believer needs you to ask God for an open door? Epaphras could not travel to Colossae, but he could wrestle for it in prayer. The Spirit may be giving you a person or a place to carry before God like that — steadily, watchfully, thankfully — until the door opens."},{"title":"Grace and Salt","body":"The Spirit listens to how you speak — at work, online, at home, to people who do not share your faith. Are your words gracious, or sharp? Are they salty, or tasteless and empty? Do you speak at people, or do you listen and answer each one? Paul says, “Let your speech always be with grace, seasoned with salt” (v. 6). Ask the Spirit to show you one conversation this week that could become an open door, and to fill your mouth with words that make Christ attractive."},{"title":"Finish What He Gave You","body":"The Spirit is speaking to every Archippus: “Take heed to the ministry which you have received in the Lord, that you fulfill it” (v. 17). Perhaps you started something God gave you — a calling, a ministry, a prayer burden, a person to disciple — and you have drifted. Perhaps, like Demas, the present world has become more attractive. Or perhaps, like Mark, you failed once and believe you are disqualified. The Spirit says: take it up again. What the Lord gave you is still yours to fulfil."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Prayer Check","body":"List the people and places you have prayed for in the past week. Is your prayer steady, watchful and thankful (v. 2)? Add one missionary or pastor to your list and pray specifically for an open door for the word (v. 3)."},{"title":"The Speech Check","body":"Think back over your conversations with unbelievers this week. Were your words gracious? Were they salty, or empty? Did you listen enough to know how to answer each one (v. 6)? Write down one conversation you would like to have again, and how you would answer differently."},{"title":"The Faithfulness Check","body":"Read the names in verses 7–17. Which of these people are you most like: the faithful courier, the restored failure, the wrestler in prayer, the host, the one who drifted? Ask God what ministry you have received from Him, and whether you are fulfilling it (v. 17)."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"The Open Door List","body":"Make a short list of five people or ministries who are carrying the gospel in hard places: a missionary, a pastor, a persecuted church, a friend who shares Christ at work. For each, write one ‘open door’ prayer based on verses 3–4: that God would give them opportunity, clarity and boldness. Pray through the list every day for a week. Then, if you can, send one of them a short message telling them you are praying — like Epaphras, striving for them in prayer."},{"title":"A Tychicus Visit","body":"Tychicus was sent to bring news and comfort hearts (v. 8). This week, be a Tychicus to someone. Choose a believer who is discouraged, isolated or new in faith. Visit them, call them or write to them. Bring real news of what God is doing, listen to their circumstances, and speak words of grace seasoned with salt. Before you go, pray that the Lord would use you to comfort their heart. Afterward, write down what God showed you through that encounter."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, You are my Master in heaven. Make me just and fair to everyone under my care, and faithful to everyone I serve. Teach me to continue steadfastly in prayer, watchful and thankful, not giving up when the answer is slow. Open doors for Your word — for missionaries, pastors and believers in chains, and for me in my own place. Give clarity and boldness to those who speak the mystery of Christ. Help me walk in wisdom toward those who do not yet know You, and to make the most of every opportunity You give. Let my speech always be gracious and seasoned with salt, so that I may know how to answer each person. Thank You for the faithful people who carried the gospel to me. Make me like Tychicus, bringing comfort; like Epaphras, wrestling in prayer; like Luke, faithful to the end. Keep me from loving this present world like Demas. Thank You that, as with Mark, failure is not final with You. Show me the ministry I have received from You, and give me grace to fulfil it. Your grace began my story, and Your grace will finish it. Grace be with Your people. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Mark's Restoration","ref":"Acts 15:36-41","relatedVerses":"v. 10","summary":"When Paul proposed revisiting the churches of the first journey, Barnabas wanted to take his cousin Mark. Paul refused, because Mark had “withdrawn from them in Pamphylia, and didn’t go with them to do the work” (Acts 15:38). The disagreement was so sharp that the two great missionaries separated: Barnabas took Mark to Cyprus, and Paul chose Silas. It is one of the saddest moments in Acts. Yet years later, in Colossians 4:10, Mark is with Paul in Rome, and Paul tells the churches to welcome him. In his last letter, Paul asks for him: “Take Mark, and bring him with you, for he is useful to me for service” (2 Timothy 4:11). The man who once quit became the author of the Gospel of Mark. Barnabas's patience and God's grace restored him, and Paul himself was big enough to change his mind."},{"title":"Demas Who Loved This Present World","ref":"2 Timothy 4:9-11","relatedVerses":"v. 14","summary":"In Colossians 4:14 and Philemon, Demas is listed among Paul's fellow workers, side by side with Luke. He had travelled with the apostle, seen the gospel's power and shared the hardships of imprisonment. But in Paul's last letter, written from a colder, lonelier prison, the news is sad: “for Demas left me, having loved this present world, and went to Thessalonica” (2 Timothy 4:10). And then: “Only Luke is with me” (2 Timothy 4:11). The two names that stood together in Colossians had gone in opposite directions. Demas's story is a sober reminder that closeness to ministry does not guarantee faithfulness, and that love for the present world can quietly pull a believer away. It is the reverse of Mark's story, and both are in Scripture for our instruction."},{"title":"Onesimus Comes Home","ref":"Philemon 1-25","relatedVerses":"v. 9; 3:11; 4:1","summary":"Onesimus was a slave in the household of Philemon, a believer in Colossae. He ran away — possibly having stolen from his master — and somehow ended up near Paul in prison, where he came to faith in Christ. Paul grew to love him like a son. Rather than keeping him, Paul sent him back to Colossae with Tychicus, carrying a short personal letter to Philemon. In it Paul asks Philemon to receive Onesimus no longer just as a slave but as a beloved brother, and even to welcome him as he would welcome Paul himself, with any debt charged to Paul's account. In Colossians 4:9, the whole church hears Onesimus introduced as ‘the faithful and beloved brother, who is one of you’. The truth of 3:11 — that in Christ there is no slave or free — walked through the door of the Colossian church in person."}]}],"review":{"type":"finalReview","book":"Colossians","titleEmojis":"👑✝️","bigPicture":{"subtitle":"Christ Is All — and You Are Complete in Him","thesis":"If we had to summarize the Book of Colossians in one overarching thought, it is this: Jesus Christ is supreme over everything, and because all the fullness of God dwells in Him, you need nothing added to Him. You are complete in Christ — so set your mind on Him, and live every part of life in His name.","paragraphs":["Start with the problem, because Paul did. Colossae was a small, fading town in the Lycus valley, overshadowed by its wealthier neighbours Laodicea and Hierapolis. Paul had never visited it. The church had been planted by Epaphras, one of their own, who had heard the gospel from Paul in Ephesus and carried it home. Now Epaphras had come to Paul in prison with good news and bad news. The good news was their faith and love. The bad news was a teaching that sounded deeply spiritual: a mixture of human philosophy, Jewish food laws and festivals, worship of angels, visions, and harsh treatment of the body. It did not deny Christ. It simply said Christ was not enough.","Paul's answer is the highest view of Christ in all his letters. Before he says a word about the false teaching, he sings a hymn: Christ “is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of all creation” (1:15). “For by him all things were created, in the heavens and on the earth, things visible and things invisible, whether thrones or dominions or principalities or powers; all things have been created through him, and for him” (1:16). “He is before all things, and in him all things are held together” (1:17). He is the Creator, the Sustainer, the Head of the church, the firstborn from the dead, the Reconciler of all things, “that in all things he might have the preeminence” (1:18). If He is this great, what could anyone add to Him?","That is exactly the argument of chapter 2. “For in him all the fullness of the Deity dwells bodily, and in him you are made full” (2:9–10). Believers have been circumcised in Him, buried and raised with Him, made alive and forgiven. The record of debt against them has been cancelled: “he has taken it out of the way, nailing it to the cross” (2:14). The powers the false teachers feared have been defeated: “having stripped the principalities and the powers, he made a show of them openly, triumphing over them in it” (2:15). So rules about food and festivals are only shadows; Christ is the substance. Human regulations look wise but cannot change the heart. The Colossians needed nothing more. They needed to go deeper into what they already had.","And then the letter turns to life. Because you were raised with Christ, “seek the things that are above, where Christ is, seated on the right hand of God” (3:1). Put off the old self and put on the new, clothed in compassion, kindness, humility, gentleness and patience, bound together by love. Let the peace of Christ rule, let His word dwell richly, and “Whatever you do, in word or in deed, do all in the name of the Lord Jesus” (3:17). That reaches into marriage, parenting and daily work, into prayer, speech to outsiders and friendships in ministry. The supreme Christ of chapter 1 becomes the Lord of the kitchen, the workshop and the conversation. That is Colossians: Christ is all, and in all."]},"map":{"intro":"Colossians is the twelfth book of the New Testament and one of Paul's four “prison letters,” alongside Ephesians, Philippians and Philemon. It was carried by Tychicus, together with Ephesians and the short letter to Philemon, and sent to a small church in the Lycus valley in the Roman province of Asia, about a hundred miles inland from Ephesus.","sections":[{"title":"The Twin of Ephesians","body":"Colossians and Ephesians were written at the same time, sent with the same messenger and share much of the same language: the old self and the new, Christ as Head of the body, the household codes, the psalms, hymns and spiritual songs. But they have different emphases. Ephesians focuses on the church, the body of Christ, and God's plan to unite all things in Him. Colossians focuses on Christ, the Head, and His absolute supremacy. Ephesians says, ‘be filled with the Spirit’; Colossians says, ‘let the word of Christ dwell in you richly’ — and both produce the same singing, thankful, submissive church. Read together, they show the Head and the body as one."},{"title":"After Philippians: From Christ's Descent to Christ's Throne","body":"Philippians showed Christ humbling Himself, taking the form of a servant and becoming obedient to death on a cross, and then being exalted to the name above every name. Colossians starts where that exaltation ends: with Christ as Creator and Sustainer of everything, the One in whom all fullness dwells. Philippians teaches believers to have the mind of Christ; Colossians teaches them that they are complete in Christ. Philippians is joy in Christ whatever the circumstances; Colossians is sufficiency in Christ whatever the competing teaching. Both were written from chains, and both put Christ at the centre of everything."},{"title":"A Letter for Every Generation of ‘Christ Plus’","body":"The exact mixture at Colossae has gone, but its shape keeps returning. Every generation produces teachings that do not deny Jesus but quietly add to Him: Christ plus a special experience, Christ plus a secret knowledge, Christ plus a diet or calendar, Christ plus spiritual techniques, Christ plus fear of spiritual powers. Colossians is the church's standing answer. It does not fight these ideas mainly by arguing against each detail. It fights them by showing Christ so clearly that additions look small. Whenever believers feel incomplete or anxious about the spiritual realm, Colossians says: look again at Him."}],"roadAhead":{"intro":"The Road Ahead: Colossians ended with Christ enthroned at God's right hand and with the promise, “When Christ, our life, is revealed, then you will also be revealed with him in glory” (3:4). The next two books take that promise and look at it closely. 1 and 2 Thessalonians are letters about the return of Christ — how to live while waiting for Him, and how not to be shaken while He delays.","books":[{"book":"1 Thessalonians","connection":"Colossians lifted your eyes to where Christ is now; 1 Thessalonians lifts them to where He is coming from. It is one of Paul's earliest letters, written to a young church just months old, persecuted almost from the start. They had “turned to God from idols, to serve a living and true God” (1 Thessalonians 1:9), and now they waited “for his Son from heaven” (1 Thessalonians 1:10). But some of their members had died, and they were grieving: would those believers miss Christ's coming? Paul answers with one of the clearest passages on the return of Jesus: “For the Lord himself will descend from heaven with a shout, with the voice of the archangel, and with God’s trumpet. The dead in Christ will rise first” (1 Thessalonians 4:16), and then, “So we will be with the Lord forever” (1 Thessalonians 4:17). Colossians said your life is hidden with Christ; 1 Thessalonians tells you the day the hiding ends. And like Colossians, it turns hope into daily life: “Rejoice always. Pray without ceasing. In everything give thanks” (1 Thessalonians 5:16–18). Watch for how the hope of His coming produces holiness, love and work."},{"book":"2 Thessalonians","connection":"If Colossians warned against people adding to Christ, 2 Thessalonians warns against people confusing the church about Christ's return. Someone had told the Thessalonians, perhaps through a forged letter, that the day of the Lord had already come. Paul writes to steady them: “not to be quickly shaken in your mind, and not be troubled, either by spirit, or by word, or by letter as if from us, saying that the day of Christ has already come” (2 Thessalonians 2:2). Certain things must happen first, including “the rebellion” and the revealing of “the man of sin” (2 Thessalonians 2:3). Meanwhile some believers had stopped working, waiting idly for the end. Paul is blunt: “If anyone will not work, don’t let him eat” (2 Thessalonians 3:10). This echoes Colossians 3:23, where work is done heartily for the Lord. And he ends with encouragement: “But you, brothers, don’t be weary in doing what is right” (2 Thessalonians 3:13). Watch for how true hope makes believers steady, faithful and hard-working, not anxious or idle."}]}},"themesMastered":[{"title":"The Supremacy of Christ","anchorRef":"Colossians 1:18","body":"“He is the head of the body, the assembly, who is the beginning, the firstborn from the dead; that in all things he might have the preeminence” (1:18). The hymn of 1:15–20 is the heart of the letter. Christ is the image of the invisible God: if you want to know what God is like, look at Jesus. He is the Creator of all things, including every throne and power in the unseen realm. He holds everything together right now. He is the Head of the church and the first to rise from the dead. Every other theme in Colossians flows from this one. Because He is supreme, nothing can rival Him, nothing needs to be added to Him, and every part of your life belongs to Him."},{"title":"Complete in Christ","anchorRef":"Colossians 2:9-10","body":"“For in him all the fullness of the Deity dwells bodily, and in him you are made full” (2:9–10). The false teachers said the Colossians were missing something. Paul says that in Christ they had everything. They were circumcised in Him with a circumcision not made with hands, buried and raised with Him, made alive and forgiven. Their spiritual growth would not come from going beyond Christ but from going deeper into Him: “As therefore you received Christ Jesus, the Lord, walk in him, rooted and built up in him, and established in the faith” (2:6–7). You do not need a second Saviour or a secret supplement. You need more of the One you already have."},{"title":"The Cross That Cancelled the Debt and Defeated the Powers","anchorRef":"Colossians 2:14-15","body":"The cross did two great things in Colossians. First, it dealt with our guilt: God was “wiping out the handwriting in ordinances which was against us; and he has taken it out of the way, nailing it to the cross” (2:14). The debt you could never repay has been cancelled, and the document is gone. Second, it dealt with the powers: “having stripped the principalities and the powers, he made a show of them openly, triumphing over them in it” (2:15). The spiritual forces the Colossians feared were led in Christ's victory parade. Believers do not need to appease or fear them; they need to stand in Christ, who has already won."},{"title":"Raised With Christ: Put Off, Put On","anchorRef":"Colossians 3:1-4","body":"“If then you were raised together with Christ, seek the things that are above, where Christ is, seated on the right hand of God” (3:1). Holiness in Colossians is not achieved by rules, which have no power against the flesh, but by union with Christ in His death and resurrection. You died, and your life is hidden with Him. So you put to death what belongs to the old life, put away anger, malice and lies, and put on the new self, being renewed in the image of the Creator. In that new humanity “Christ is all, and in all” (3:11). And over all the new garments you put on love, which holds everything together."},{"title":"Everything in His Name","anchorRef":"Colossians 3:17","body":"“Whatever you do, in word or in deed, do all in the name of the Lord Jesus, giving thanks to God the Father, through him” (3:17). The supremacy of Christ is not only a doctrine for theologians; it reaches into marriages, families and workplaces. Wives and husbands, children and fathers, servants and masters all live under the same Lord. Work becomes worship: “And whatever you do, work heartily, as for the Lord, and not for men” (3:23). Prayer, speech to unbelievers, friendships and ministries — all are lived in His name. Thanksgiving runs through the whole letter because a life lived for Christ is a grateful life."}],"experientialLesson":{"intro":"How do you walk out the Book of Colossians practically moving forward? Here is your experiential blueprint:","steps":[{"step":1,"title":"Look Again at Who He Is","lesson":"The Lesson: Paul's answer to the false teaching was not a list of arguments but a fresh vision of Christ: “who is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of all creation” (1:15). Most spiritual anxiety, confusion and drift begins with a small view of Jesus. When Christ is seen as He truly is, competing voices lose their pull.","practice":"The Practice: Copy out Colossians 1:15–20 by hand, line by line. Read it aloud every morning for a week. Each day, pick one phrase — ‘image of the invisible God’, ‘all things have been created through him’, ‘in him all things are held together’, ‘the head of the body’, ‘the firstborn from the dead’, ‘having made peace through the blood of his cross’ — and spend five minutes worshipping Christ for that one truth. At the end of the week, write down how your view of Him has grown and what fears or worries looked smaller because of it."},{"step":2,"title":"Cross Out the Additions","lesson":"The Lesson: “For in him all the fullness of the Deity dwells bodily, and in him you are made full” (2:9–10). Every ‘Christ plus’ teaching robs believers of the joy and freedom of being complete in Him, and keeps them anxious, judging others or trying to earn what they already have.","practice":"The Practice: Write down anything you have felt you need, beyond Christ, to be fully accepted by God or to feel spiritually secure — a practice, an experience, a rule, a performance, a level of knowledge. Hold each item up to Colossians 2:9–15. Thank God for anything that is a good help in following Christ, but cross out anything you have been treating as an addition to Him. Then write over the page, ‘In him you are made full.’ Keep it where you will see it, especially when you feel spiritually inadequate."},{"step":3,"title":"Live One Day Fully in His Name","lesson":"The Lesson: The supreme Christ is Lord of ordinary life. “Whatever you do, in word or in deed, do all in the name of the Lord Jesus” (3:17), and “whatever you do, work heartily, as for the Lord, and not for men” (3:23). Colossians refuses to divide life into sacred and secular.","practice":"The Practice: Choose one ordinary day and live it consciously in Christ's name. In the morning, read 3:1–4 and set your mind on Him. Before each task, say, ‘Lord, this is for You.’ At home, practise one of the instructions of 3:18–21. At work, serve heartily whether or not anyone is watching. In conversation, let your speech be gracious and seasoned with salt (4:6). Give thanks at every meal. In the evening, write down what changed. Then repeat the day once a week until it becomes the shape of your life."}]},"finalDeclaration":"“Father, I close this letter written by a prisoner about a King, and I declare: Jesus Christ is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of all creation. By Him all things were created — in heaven and on earth, visible and invisible, thrones and dominions, principalities and powers. All things were created through Him and for Him. He is before all things, and in Him all things hold together. He is the Head of the body, the church, the firstborn from the dead, and in everything He has the first place. All Your fullness dwells in Him bodily, and in Him I am made complete. I need nothing added to Him. I will not be carried off by empty philosophy or the traditions of men. As I received Christ Jesus the Lord, so I will walk in Him, rooted and built up in Him, established in the faith and overflowing with thanksgiving. I was dead in my trespasses, and You made me alive with Him and forgave me all my sins. The record of debt that stood against me was nailed to the cross. Every principality and power has been disarmed and put to open shame. I will not live under shadows when I have the substance. I have been raised with Christ, so I set my mind on the things above, where Christ is seated at Your right hand. My life is hidden with Christ in You, and when He appears I will appear with Him in glory. I put to death what belongs to my old life, and I put on the new self, renewed in His image. I clothe myself with compassion, kindness, humility, gentleness and patience, I forgive as He forgave me, and above all I put on love. The peace of Christ rules in my heart, and His word dwells in me richly. In my home and in my work, I serve the Lord Christ. I will continue steadfastly in prayer, walk in wisdom toward outsiders, and let my speech be full of grace. I will fulfil the ministry I have received in the Lord. Whatever I do, in word or deed, I will do it all in the name of the Lord Jesus, giving thanks to You through Him. Christ is all, and in all. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”"}});
